\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  CROSS-Euro turns the tables on yen, for now. \b0\par\par\uc2 683 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 1999\par 06:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 2 (Reuters) - The euro staged a rare rally on Friday, recovering three yen and capturing an important chart barrier in the process. Traders said the turnaround began overnight in Europe as heavy buying emerged from U.S. investment banks and Japanese life insurers rumoured to be increasing their portfolio holdings of European assets. There was also talk European fund managers were booking some profits on their Japanese equity holdings ahead of the end of the third quarter. \par \par \uc2  European investors have been big buyers of Japanese stocks this year as an apparent recovery in the economy encouraged them to rebuild long underweight portfolios. \par \par \uc2  This profit-taking view was borne out in part by gains seen in the Swiss franc and sterling against the yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  That carried the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from a low of 114.85 yen to test resistance at 117.05, which was duly broken this morning as stop-loss buying was triggered. \par \par \uc2  Dealers also reported talk a hedge fund was massively short of euro/yen and was either taking profits or being forced to cover some of its exposure by buying euros. \par \par \uc2  All of which was enough to lift the euro to 117.61/83 yen by 0415 GMT from 116.52 late in New York and 115.12 this time here on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The bounce went some way to improving a dire technical position but analysts cautioned that as yet it was merely a correction in a long-term downtrend. \par \par \uc2  They saw scope to test resistance in the 117.80/90 area for a break to 118.40, which would be a 38 percent retracement of the fall from 124.25 in early August to this week's lifetime low of 114.77. \par \par \uc2  But they added that to counter the trend the euro would have to climb above 119.30 yen, the level where its last bounce failed in August. \par \par \uc2  Euro demand was also encouraged by weakness in U.S. asset markets, which were fretting anew about the risk of another Federal Reserve rate hike this year. \par \par \uc2  This was making assets in Europe, and Japan, look relatively more attractive and was supporting the euro and yen at the expense of the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The euro had pushed up to $1.0682/87 this afternoon from $1.0593/96 late here on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile the yen was hindered by another bout of market nerves over possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers reported vague rumours that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been spotted buying dollar/yen overnight, though few credited the talk. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said on Friday that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if needed and would not have to wait for a meeting of the Group of Seven (G7) nations later this month to seek cooperation from its partners to rectify a premature rise in the yen. \par \par \uc2  Dealers also noted that this was a holiday weekend for U.S. markets and that the Bank of Japan had used such occasions in the past to catch a thin Monday-market with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The yen also retreated against sterling to stand at 177.01/13 yen from a three-year high of 174.06 on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Traders reported some heavy buying on this cross, which in part was profit-taking on short sterling positions and part Japanese investment flows into Britain. \par \par \uc2  The pound had also bolstered its technical position by breaking resistance at 176.95 yen and analysts looked for a run on to 177.77 now, though many thought the rally would falter there and the downtrend resume. \par \par \uc2  It was much the same for the Swiss franc cross which having touched a four-year low of 71.71 yen on Tuesday had veered higher to reach 73.68/73 today. \par \par \uc2  Again it had cleared important resistance at 73.00/10 and 73.60 yen which augured well for a push to 74.65 in the near term, analysts said. ((Wayne Cole, Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 8584 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9301buu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
