\par\par \b \uc2  POLL-Yen strength seen unsustainable. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Penny MacRae \par \uc2 679 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 1999\par 12:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 3 (Reuters) - Yen bulls beware - the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's near 10 percent surge against the dollar since July is unsustainable, economists surveyed by Reuters warned. \par \par \uc2  Japan's economic recovery will disappoint investors in coming months, they said, leading investors to push down the yen against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "We think the market has an overly rosy view of the Japanese economy," said Sal Gautieri of Bank of Montreal in Toronto, who expects Japanese growth this year to be at the most one percent. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par According to the median forecast of 52 economists polled on Aug 31-Sept 3, the dollar\tab   will be at 110.00 yen in one month and 115.00 in three, six and 12 months. The range was 95 to 145 on a 12-month horizon.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Since July, the yen has powered nearly 10 percent higher against the dollar as investors have piled into Japanese assets as a recovery play.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Gautieri said the yen's exuberance had been exaggerated by strong capital flows into Japanese asset markets. "Once the market's view is unrealised, this will take the yen lower," said Gautieri who sees the dollar at 133 yen in 12 months.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar was trading on Friday at 109.85 yen with fear of Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  keeping the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  off seven-month lows set this week.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Just last August, the dollar was trading at 146 yen. But an improving Japanese economic outlook after years of dismal growth is siphoning investment away from the United States toward Japan. Expectations of slowing U.S. growth have added to pressure on the dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Still, scepticism abounds.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The yen's strength is just a temporary appreciation because of expectations of a positive rebound in domestic growth," said Mia Rigo of Bank of America. "But we don't think this is something longterm - they still have not addressed a lot of the problems."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Others who believe the yen's prospects of further gains are limited argued Japan's authorities will stop the yen moving higher to prevent it choking off the country's nascent recovery.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The Japanese authorities will not tolerate an overly strong yen for a sustained period of time and this will produce a weaker yen," said AIB senior economist Pat O'Sullivan in Dublin.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Financial markets have been extremely nervous about potential \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  action to tame the yen's advance. But while there has been a chorus of dollar-supportive rhetoric from Japanese officials, there has been no detectable \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "We are getting a lot of verbal \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  but so far they are doing absolutely nothing," said O'Sullivan.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Economists said without a further easing of monetary policy by the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would be ineffective.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par While respondents supplied only dollar/yen forecasts, implicit cross rates showed the yen sliding to 132.25 against the euro in 12 months. The euro was trading around 117.63 yen against the euro on Friday after falling to lifetime lows against the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  earlier in the week.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Some economists remained yen bulls, however, with Adam Chester of Halifax Plc forecasting the dollar at 100 yen in 12 months time.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par He said international investors are still "heavily short of their benchmarks in the yen and a rebalancing in portfolios towards Japan should be yen supportive.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Another bull was Kirit Shah of Sanwa International who saw the dollar at 95 yen in 12 months. While he expected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at the 105 yen level, he predicted it would be ineffective.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Among factors driving yen strength were "confidence in the financial markets as the Nikkei heads to 20,000-22,000 over the coming months," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "With Japan holding a strong trump card in term of liquid savings and the world witnessing a drain in liquidity as the Fed tightens...trends in capital flows and savings imbalances are now significantly dictating valuations in both bond and exchange rate markets."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Polling unit +44 171 542 7958, fax +44 171 542 4939, penny.macrae@reuters.com).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1999.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv93015g9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
