\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar shoots higher in US as inflation fears lift. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Elizabeth Lazarowitz \par \uc2 716 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 1999\par 16:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Sept 3 (Reuters) - The dollar rallied on Friday after soft U.S. jobs data soothed market fears that the Federal Reserve will raise rates again soon, sending U.S. asset markets shooting higher. \par \par \uc2  Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  tumbled three-quarters of a cent against the dollar shortly after the closely watched August employment report, and the dollar jumped a quarter of a yen against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The dollar will go higher because domestic financial markets are taking heart from the (employment) numbers," said Ram Bhagavatula, chief economist at Natwest Global Financial Markets. \par \par \uc2  The jobs report showed U.S. payrolls rose by 124,000 in August, well below Wall Street expectations of a 220,000 rise, following an upwardly revised 338,000 in July. \par \par \uc2  "All of those suggest that the Fed may not tighten on October 5...and suddenly all of the markets are rallying. That's typically good for the dollar," Bhagavatula said. \par \par \uc2  Unemployment slipped slightly to 4.2 percent in August, in line with projections, while average hourly earnings rose a tepid 0.2 percent, easing recent fears of wage inflation. \par \par \uc2  Buoying the dollar, the 30-year U.S. Treasury bond shot up by more than a full point, bringing its yield down near 6.00 percent, while the Dow Jones industrial average rallied by some 175 points shortly after opening. \par \par \uc2  By 0940 EDT/1340 GMT, the euro had tumbled sharply to $1.0590/01 from $1.0675/80 in early U.S. dealing and $1.0680/87 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's gains against the yen were more muted. After initially popping above 110 yen, the dollar eased back a touch but was still firmer at 109.91/96 yen against 109.80/90 in early U.S. trading and well above 109.10/20 in late Thursday dealing. \par \par \uc2  Market players had been worried that signs of continued robust economic growth and price pressures in recent U.S. economic data had put the inflation-fighting Federal Reserve on high alert. \par \par \uc2  Higher interest rates can be positive for a nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by attracting investors to higher-yielding assets, but analysts say the dollar has recently been hurt by fears that a tightening would put a damper on U.S. stocks. \par \par \uc2  "Now we could have not as high rates but a better climate for U.S. asset markets and that tends to attract capital," said David Horner, senior financial strategist at Merrill Lynch. \par \par \uc2  "Basically, this takes the pressure off for now," he added. \par \par \uc2  The financial markets' cheerful reaction to the data also eased \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers' jitters over Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to tame the yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The market has been extremely wary of potential \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action to crimp the yen's recent strength, which pushed the dollar to a seven-month low near 108.62 on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said thin market conditions ahead of the U.S. Labour Day weekend would make for choppy trading. But they also might make for perfect conditions for Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , since thin markets are easier to move, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "The Japanese have often \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in thin markets before," said Ben Strauss, vice president at Bank Julius Baer. That made dealers reluctant to leave positions exposed, he said. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials' stepped-up efforts also helped damp the yen's fire on Friday. Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said Tokyo will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets if necessary, and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not need to wait for a meeting of Group of Seven leaders to see cooperation to tame the yen. \par \par \uc2  Overnight, the euro remained capped below Thursday's $1.0730 high after mixed data from Germany, the euro zone's largest economy, clouded the outlook for a turnaround in European growth. \par \par \uc2  The Reuters German August services index rose to a seasonally adjusted 56.52 from 55.88 in the prior month, showing the service sector expanded at the fastest pace in a year. But German July wholesale sales fell a nominal 3.7 percent and fell a real 3.0 percent year-on-year. \par \par \uc2  In other dealing, the dollar was also firmer against the Swiss franc and the British pound. \par \par \uc2  (( - N.A. Treasury Desk, 212-859-1607)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv93014yd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
