\par\par \b \uc2  Tokyo Stocks End 1% Lower, Erasing Half Of Wednesday's Rally \b0\par\par\uc2 802 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 September 1999\par 09:05\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Tokyo stocks closed lower Thursday on profit-taking following Wednesday's 2% rise. The dollar remained at an eight-month low against against the yen in late Asian trading amid steady demand for the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from U.S. investors. \par \par \uc2  Japan's blue-chip Nikkei 225 index fell 171.23 points, or 1%, to close at 17631.25. Wednesday, the average rose 365.92 points, or 2.1%. Losers edged winners 9 to 4 while 612.6 million shares were traded. \par \par \uc2  Stock prices moved lower as investors locked in profits following Wednesday's sharp gains. But traders said general market sentiment remained resilient, partly because of an overnight rebound on Wall Street and amid hopes for an economic recovery in Japan. \par \par \uc2  "Despite the yen's strength, the market remains comparatively strong," said Kazunori Jinnai, deputy general manager at Daiwa Securities SB Capital Markets. \par \par \uc2  Wednesday in New York, the Dow Jones Industrial Average rose 108.60, or 1%, to 10937.88. \par \par \uc2  Many bank stocks rallied on active buying across the financial sector by foreign investors, and those gains helped lift the Tokyo Stock Price Index despite falls in a broad majority of its components. \par \par \uc2  To the surprise of many, the yen's continuing advance didn't hurt market averages as much as the profit-taking. Some normally \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -susceptible issues showed firmness, though many exporters did fall. \par \par \uc2  In \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealings, the dollar continued to lose ground as U.S. funds bought yen to invest in Japanese stocks. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was quoted at 108.85 yen, lower than 109.08 yen late Wednesday in New York. The dollar last traded in Asia at the 108-yen level on Jan. 12. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's fall also derived from the growing notion that Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the U.S. Federal Reserve were unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's advance, traders said. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers did not refer to either \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or yen strength in an interview published Wednesday in the USA Today newspaper. Traders took that as a sign \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not take place. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa told a regular news conference Thursday morning that Japan would take steps against rapid and disruptive movements on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Japanese government officials have frequently warned of possible dollar-supportive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but no such action has been seen for the past month. \par \par \uc2  "Dealers are just playing catch as the Nikkei stays range-bound amid a lack of direction in the near-term market outlook," said Kazunori Shimono, manager of equity trading at Tokyo-Mitsubishi Personal Securities. \par \par \uc2  Mid- to low-priced domestic demand-dependent stocks generally faced profit-taking, while selected export-oriented stocks and some other blue chips were bought by foreign and local institutional investors, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "Japan bullish players bought exporters with superior technology which would offset the impact of the yen's appreciation," Shimono said. \par \par \uc2  Shipbuilders tumbled on the stronger yen, after Kawasaki Heavy Industries downgraded Wednesday its earnings estimates for the first half of the current fiscal year due in part to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent rise. \par \par \uc2  Kawasaki Heavy Industries fell 27 yen to 236 yen. Mitsubishi Heavy Industries, Hitachi Zosen and Mitsui Engineering and Shipbuilding all finished lower. \par \par \uc2  In the food sector, Asahi Soft Drinks fell 155 yen to 2,000 yen on active profit-taking on poor business prospects, traders said. The stock debuted on the first section on Tuesday. Its rival Kirin Beverage dropped 35 yen to 1,919 yen. \par \par \uc2  Asahi Breweries shed 29 yen to 1,690 yen, while Kirin Brewery slid 25 yen to 1,313 yen. \par \par \uc2  Nissan Motor rallied in the second-largest turnover on the first section after the auto maker announced Wednesday that its August domestic car sales rose 1.7% on year, its first year-on-year rise in 15 months. "The rise in sales added to hopes for drastic restructuring at the company," said Kazunori Jinnai, deputy general manager of equities at Daiwa Securities SB Capital Markets. Nissan closed up 23 yen at 690 yen. Other major car makers were also higher earlier in the day's trading on hopes for a recovery in domestic demand, but finished marginally lower. \par \par \uc2  Sony slid 200 yen to 14,150 yen while Fujitsu fell 10 yen to 3,250 yen. Oki Electric soared 28 yen to 570 yen while Sharp edged up 57 yen to 1,749 yen. NEC and Toshiba fell, while Hitachi ended up. \par \par \uc2  Shares of Hikari Tsushin Inc., a cellular phone subscription agency, debuted Thursday on the Tokyo Stock Exchange's first section at 57,000 yen, unchanged from their close on the over-the-counter market Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1999 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329dv9203lxq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
