\par\par \b \uc2  ECB Watch: -2: No Strong Argument For Variable Rate \b0\par\par\uc2  By G. Thomas Sims \par \uc2 889 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 September 1999\par 07:00\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  FRANKFURT (Dow Jones)--A switch by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a variable rate refinancing \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the current fixed rate isn't likely in the cards, a senior ECB official said late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  In perhaps the clearest words yet to damp market speculation of a change in the ECB's most important monetary tool, Tommaso Padoa-Schioppa, member of the executive board of the ECB, said, "We could at some point switch, but I don't see that coming." \par \par \uc2  In a wide-ranging discussion with Frankfurt-based journalists, Padoa-Schioppa provided the closest look at ECB thinking since the bank recessed for a summer holiday in July. In addition to ECB policy, he tackled other issues important for financial markets, including the strengthening of the yen and a forthcoming meeting of finance ministers from the Group of Seven most richest nations. \par \par \uc2  Ever since ECB president Wim Duisenberg on July 15 said the ECB was considering a variable rate refinancing \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the course of ECB monetary policy has been unclear. Speculation concerning the variable rate - coupled with Duisenberg's admitting a tightening bias was "creeping" into ECB deliberations - prompted many economists to bring forward their expectations for a rate increase, now largely anticipated by the end of next March. \par \par \uc2  Padoa-Shioppa was the most explicit of ECB officials who've recently commented on the problems of a variable rate. \par \par \uc2  "I agree...a variable rate could produce an undesirable move in interest rates," Padoa-Schioppa said. Under the current regime, banks place bids for the volume of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds they'd be willing to take on at a fixed 2.5% rate. Under a variable rate, banks bid both the volume and the rate they'd be willing to pay, something proponents say could make bidding fairer for smaller banks in some countries and prevent massive overbidding in the weekly auctions. \par \par \uc2  Padoa-Schioppa dismissed changing the system to solve the overbidding problem alone. "For this reason I don't want to change the system," he said. \par \par \uc2  While Padoa-Schioppa was clear about his stance on the main refinancing \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he was vague on the future path of interest rates. He wouldn't comment on statements earlier Wednesday by the new president of the Deutsche Bundesbank and ECB council member, Ernst Welteke. Welteke said there's no need for a change in official interest rates in the absence of inflationary dangers and only "moderate" money supply growth. \par \par \uc2  "There's not much point speculating what the governing council will do next week" when it meets on Sept. 9, Padoa-Schioppa said. \par \par \uc2  The ECB official said the ECB isn't under pressure to follow rate rises by the U.S. Federal Reserve. He also said the "most appropriate" way to interpret Duisenberg's comments of a tightening bias creeping into ECB thinking is that "the process of creeping in may take months." \par \par \uc2  Padoa-Schioppa didn't go into much detail on the euro-zone economy, other than to say the second-half growth pick up suggested by recent data is in line with the ECB's expectations. \par \par \uc2  Padoa-Schioppa said recent strong growth in broad M3 money supply data "need close scrutiny." Figures revealed a week ago showed June M3 growing 5.6%, above the ECB's rough 4.5% guideline for growth. \par \par \uc2  "We need to understand this recent data and we know little about future data," he said. "This was one month and we are keen to see next month." \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Money supply forms one of two pillars of the ECB's policy strategy.\par The other is a broad array of inflation indicators.\par Yen Strengthening Not Problem For ECB, But Maybe For Japan\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Asked about the recent strengthening of the yen against the euro,\par Padoa-Schioppa, who heads the ECB's international relations department,\par said the stronger Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  doesn't impact the external value of\par the euro, which the ECB likes to measure against a basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In June, the ECB \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on behalf of the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stem the\par yen's rise. Earlier Wednesday, the dollar slid below Y109 to a yet\par another seven-and-a-half-month low, which has sparked fears of\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "We know the Japanese economy is probably in the early phase of\par recovery awaited for very long after five consecutive quarters of\par declines," he said. "Japanese officials think a strong yen shouldn't be\par an impediment to recovery. That is what we know of Japanese officials."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On Monday evening, Padoa-Schioppa took part in a planning meeting of\par representatives from the G-7, including Haruhiko Kuroda, Japan's Vice\par Finance Minister for International Affairs.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Padoa-Schioppa provided a peek of what can be expected from a meeting\par of G-7 finance ministers later in September.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "On the whole the picture for the financial and real economy is much\par less worrisome than a year ago," Padoa-Schioppa said when asked to\par comment on the G-7 planning meetings he attended in Berlin earlier this\par week.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par He said the risks to this outlook include Latin America, the U.S.\par economic slowdown and problems stemming from the year 2000 changeover.\par -By G. Thomas Sims; 4969 29725 500; tom.sims@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 02-09-99\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0500GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1103 4270 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv9206jsv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
