\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  CROSS-Euro bows low as yen rules the crosses. \b0\par\par\uc2 741 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 September 1999\par 07:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 2 (Reuters) - The euro/yen rate kept a precarious perch above the week's lifetime lows on Thursday but dealers said the failure to manage even a modest bounce meant all the risks were still to the downside. \par \par \uc2  The same was true on a number of other crosses, with sterling at a three-year trough and the Australian dollar making eight months lows on the yen. \par \par \uc2  After cratering at 114.77 yen on Tuesday the euro clawed back to 116.50, but crucially could not sustain the gains and relapsed to 115.12/30 by 0500 GMT. \par \par \uc2  The euro was pressured by fresh strength in the yen against the dollar, where it hit a new seven-month high, as well as by ongoing expectations of offshore investment flows into Japanese assets. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the euro had put in a better performance on the dollar, rising to $1.0585/90 from $1.0460 at the start of the week, which was providing some support on the crosses. \par \par \uc2  Yen bulls were also keeping a wary eye out for possible Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but were reasonably confident \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not be able to enlist the assistance of the Europeans on euro/yen rates. \par \par \uc2  European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Directorate Member Tommaso Padoa-Schioppa reinforced this view on Wednesday by saying the ECB did not look at the euro's exchange rate against the yen in particular but at a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  As such, developments in the yen just fed into a general assessment of euro exchange rate developments, he said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said Japanese exporters and institutions still appeared to have a need to sell to hedge their euro exposure while the speculative side of the market was being driven by bearish technical indicators. \par \par \uc2  "While under 115.50, the risks are highly skewed toward a retest of the major bear trend low at 114.80," said an analyst at Standard and Poor's MMS. \par \par \uc2  "Continuation of the fall below there will potentially target 113.00/30 near the base of the bear channel. With this in mind, the bias favours selling upticks." \par \par \uc2  Others were drawing their downside targets from the old mark/yen cross which was last down at these levels in early 1995. It then made a post-war low around 57.50 yen, which corresponds to about 112.50 yen for the euro, and this was the main target of many analysts. \par \par \uc2  Reaching such a level would argue for a substantial correction to the upside or at least a pause for consolidation, but that could even extend to resistance around 117.00 without changing the overall downtrend, they added. \par \par \uc2  The yen had also made technically significant breaks on other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week, including sterling and the Australian dollar. \par \par \uc2  The pound slumped through support around 175.70 yen on Tuesday to reach 174.14, its lowest reading since October 1996. An attempt to bounce from there was quashed by fresh selling at 176.60 and it had sagged to 174.33/45 on Thursday afternoon. \par \par \uc2  The next target was the 173.22 yen trough from October 1996 and then 172.80, which marked an area of congestion from July 1996. Beyond that was a Fibonacci target at 171.32, which would mark a 61.8 percent retracement of the entire uptrend from 128.30 yen in April 1995 to the peak of 240.90 in August last year. \par \par \uc2  If that were lost, analysts were talking of scope to reach 168.00 or even 156.50 yen in the medium term. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar, meanwhile, had finally broken beneath major support at 70.00 yen and gone on to crack another important bulwark at 69.45 yen, its lowest level in eight months. \par \par \uc2  It had steadied at 69.57/67 yen in the afternoon and analysts noted the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was deeply oversold and due for a bounce. But again the overall trend was clearly downwards and all looked for a test of the Janaury trough around 68.70 yen in the short term. \par \par \uc2  A breach there would take it to territory not seen since August 1995 and analysts warned there was little in the way of chart support until 65.50 yen, which was resistance for a couple of weeks back in July 1995 ((Wayne Cole, Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 8584 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv92019ka\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
