\par\par \b \uc2  IMF Official Warns Further Gains In Yen Could Derail Japan's Economy \b0\par\par\uc2 625 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 September 1999\par 06:25\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- In the clearest signal yet that the international community is growing more concerned about the strong yen, a senior International Monetary Fund official warned Wednesday that a rapid appreciation of the yen could derail Japan's economy. \par \par \uc2  Hubert Neiss, director of the IMF's Asia and Pacific department, said in a speech in Tokyo that "a further rapid appreciation of the yen could well put an end to the recovery, as has happened before." \par \par \uc2  "This is an important risk," Neiss said. \par \par \uc2  Japan has come under increasing international pressure to revive its sluggish economy, mired in its deepest recession since the end of World War II. Japan's economy is still struggling to shake off the effects of a plunge in land prices in the early 1990s, which buried the country's banks under a mountain of failed or risky loans. The government has pumped billions of dollars into the economy in an all-out campaign to prod consumers to spend. Lackluster consumer spending is a major reason the Japanese economy hasn't been able to rescue itself. \par \par \uc2  Neiss urged the Japanese government to take steps to counter the rise of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and to ensure that fiscal policy doesn't start to work as a drag on economic growth. Significantly, Neiss included in his remarks specific policy steps that Tokyo could use to prevent the yen from rising. \par \par \uc2  He said that among the macro policy instruments available to fight the strong yen "is mainly a more aggressive monetary policy, which means strong liquidity injections by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  He added that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is "more effective if it is unsterilized because it means an expansionary monetary policy." \par \par \uc2  In unsterilized \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would sell the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and then leave those funds in the money market to create oversupply - and thus easier monetary conditions. In recent months, the Bank of Japan has in fact sopped up extra yen in the money market, thereby undermining the effectiveness of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to analysts. \par \par \uc2  The remarks by Neiss came against a backdrop of rising concerns in Japan about the dollar's trading level against the yen. In Asian trading Wednesday, the dollar was quoted around 109.40 yen - not far off the low for 1999 of 108.21 yen set in early January. As recently as early July, the dollar was trading above 120 yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's slide has spurred notions that Japan and its partners in the Group of Seven might have to resort to coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to break the trend. Thus far, monetary authorities in the other G-7 nations have downplayed the chances of joint action on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But Neiss appeared to be gently pressing the case for coordinated action Wednesday. He noted that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is "more effective if done with other central banks." \par \par \uc2  Turning to fiscal policy in Japan, Neiss said there is a risk that "Fiscal policy stimulus put in place last year will taper off and not be offset fully by an additional supplementary budget." \par \par \uc2  Regarding the yen and fiscal policy, he said, "These risks are something the government can do something about." \par \par \uc2  Despite his warnings about the yen and fiscal drag, Neiss said the IMF may upwardly revise its growth forecast for Japan this year. He noted that the IMF expects Japanese gross domestic product to expand by 0.25% in 1999. \par \par \uc2  This estimate will "probably be raised to one half a percent as things go on," he said. \par \par \uc2  Touching briefly on China's yuan, Neiss said China's government is strong enough to "keep the yuan stable." \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1999 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329dv9103kvl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
