\par\par \b \uc2  Japan repeats premature yen gains are undesirable. \b0\par\par\uc2 425 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 August 1999\par 09:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Aug 29 (Reuters) - Haruhiko Kuroda, Japan's vice finance minister for international affairs, told a Sunday meeting of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and finance ministry officials the yen's premature appreciation was undesirable, a senior Japanese official said. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda was reiterating the view that a recent rise in the yen against the U.S. dollar was too much, too soon, and was not favourable for either the Japanese or the world economy. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda was speaking at a meeting in Singapore of the Manila Framework group to discuss Asia's economic crisis. \par \par \uc2  The senior finance ministry official, who told reporters of Kuroda's comments, said no other member in the 14 economy grouping objected to the view. \par \par \uc2  The group comprises Australia, Brunei, Canada, China, Hong Kong, Indonesia, Japan, South Korea, Malaysia, New Zealand, the Philippines, Singapore, Thailand and the United States. \par \par \uc2  The official quoted Kuroda as telling the meeting that while premature appreciation was undesirable, "Japan, on the other hand, had no intention of keeping the yen low". \par \par \uc2  The yen hit a seven-month high of 110.36 to the dollar early last week as hopes grew that years of recession in Japan were giving way to recovery which could attract foreign funds. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda had not given any comment on the level of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  or whether the countries were going to discuss the yen issue in more detail, the official said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar/yen exchange rate was, however, set to top the agenda of September's G7 deputies meeting, Kuroda's predecessor, Eisuke Sakakibara told Japanese television on Sunday. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market had been in a guessing game over whether Japan and the United States were in disagreement over \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy since U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers criticised "the manipulation of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 " in July. \par \par \uc2  That was around the time \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to weaken the yen. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara said Washington now had more reason to be concerned about a weaker dollar and the convergence of the two nation's views on suitable exchange rates would be the main discussion point at the G7 deputies' meeting. \par \par \uc2  "That is his observation," the official told reporters when asked about the former vice finance ministry chief's comments. "He is now adviser to the ministry." \par \par \uc2  The official said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements before the meeting would dictate whether or not the strong yen would be on the G7 agenda. \par \par \uc2  ((Nao Nakanishi +65 870-3011; Fax +65 776-8112; Email: singapore.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv8t01p0v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
