\par\par \b \uc2  Tokyo Stocks Slip 0.4% For 4th Loss In Row After Late Sell-Off \b0\par\par\uc2 726 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 August 1999\par 09:52\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Tokyo share prices closed slightly lower Friday for their fourth tumble in a row after late selling erased early gains. \par \par \uc2  Japan's blue-chip Nikkei 225 index slipped 66.92 points, or 0.4%, to close at 17599.37, extending Thursday's 188.87-point fall. Losers edged winners 7 to 5 while 487 million shares were traded. The index has slid 634.18 points, or 3.1%, in the last four sessions. \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei average closed lower amid concern that domestic investors could start selling ahead of the end of the first half of the fiscal year on Sept. 30, said Chuck Lambert, market analyst at Jardine Fleming Securities. \par \par \uc2  Worries about the effects of a strong yen also weighed down the overall market, and many investors adopted a cautious stance. Bank shares continued to slide on profit-taking. \par \par \uc2  The market had been boosted in early trading by news Friday morning that spending by Japanese wage earners in July was up 2.6% in real terms from a year earlier. Economists surveyed had estimated on average that spending would rise 0.4% from a year ago. \par \par \uc2  Thursday on Wall Street, stocks posted broad losses amid profit-taking after Wednesday's record-setting run by blue chips. The Dow Jones Industrial Average closed down 127.59, or 1.1%, at 11198.45 after gaining nearly 43 points to a record close Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was quoted at 111.55 yen, below 111.57 yen late Thursday in New York. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was supported by talk in the market that deputy finance ministers from the Group of Seven nations are set to meet this weekend. "People are reluctant to sell the dollar aggressively ahead of the weekend with vague talk (of) a G-7 deputies meeting," said Shuji Takano, assistant manager of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at ABN AMRO Bank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Upcoming events helped bolster such speculation: Haruhiko Kuroda, Japanese vice finance minister for international affairs, is planning to attend a meeting of Asian and U.S. finance deputies on Sunday and Monday in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the yen's outlook against the dollar remains uncertain amid a tug-of-war between continued demand for the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  among global investors to invest in markets here and fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has entered the market to buy dollars in recent months to stem the yen's rapid rise against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . A stronger yen makes Japanese exports more expensive abroad and thus less competitive, cutting into earnings of export-oriented Japanese companies. \par \par \uc2  The yen's weakness Thursday in New York provided a boost to stocks of many exporters, whose overseas profits grow when the dollar strengthens. Sony gained 40 yen to 14,350 yen, Matsushita Electric Industrial added 5 yen to 2,265 yen and Toyota gained 70 yen to 3,530 yen. \par \par \uc2  DDI closed 12,000 yen higher at 740,000 yen after the company said Thursday it plans to merge the \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  of its nine personal handy-phone group companies from January. News Thursday that the company expects to suffer a parent net loss of 24 billion yen for the year through March instead of a net profit of 20.5 billion yen as it had earlier forecast didn't seem to have much impact on the share price. The sector that posted the biggest decline was banks. Industrial Bank of Japan fell 97 yen to 1,203 yen, Dai-Ichi Kangyo Bank slipped 12 yen to 1,209 and Fuji Bank shed 13 yen to 1,190. The stocks of the three banks have recently posted big gains after the banks announced they would jointly set up a holding company. \par \par \uc2  Retail stocks got a lift from the July spending data. Among the gainers were department-store operator Mitsukoshi, up 5 yen at the close at 655 yen and Ito-Yokado, 310 yen higher at 8,500. \par \par \uc2  Kawasaki Steel, up for much of the day on a Nihon Keizai Shimbun report that the company is likely to have a pretax profit in its fiscal first half through September instead of the loss it had earlier forecast, ended 3 yen lower at 253. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1999 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329dv8r03ki9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
