\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar slips off highs in U.S., Greenspan weighs. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Lida Poletz \par \uc2 610 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 August 1999\par 18:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Aug 27 (Reuters) - The dollar drifted off its highs in midday U.S. trade on Friday after Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan triggered a mild case of paranoia about whether U.S. asset values are overblown. \par \par \uc2  But the major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  failed to test the extremes of the session's ranges, and dealers said the market was tired and thin after a week of often volatile moves. \par \par \uc2  The dollar followed U.S. stocks modestly lower after Greenspan told a conference central banks must watch asset prices when setting interest rates. He avoided saying whether he thought U.S. stocks were overvalued, though. \par \par \uc2  "Reading between the lines ... Greenspan seems to be indicating that the U.S. market is overvalued, and traders are going to assume that the dollar could come under pressure if stocks sell off," said David Gilmore, partner at \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics. \par \par \uc2  The Fed earlier this week raised two key short-term interest rates by a quarter point and maintained its neutral monetery policy bias, which markets have interpreted to mean there would be no more rate rises this year. \par \par \uc2  The dollar edged off early highs to trade at 111.61/71 at 1200 EDT/1600 GMT, but it was still up from the late Thursday trading levels at 111.50/60 and seven-month lows below 111 hit earlier this week. \par \par \uc2  The dollar also slipped against the euro, which stood at $1.0460/67 at New York midday, up from $1.0444/51. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones industrial average, meanwhile, traded down around 50 points. \par \par \uc2  Trading conditions were very thin although some traders were cautious about the potential for volatility after a week which saw the buoyant yen shoot to seven-month highs against the dollar and fresh lifetime peaks versus the euro. \par \par \uc2  "It's very quiet. There's a holiday in London on Monday so they've got one foot out the door already," said Rick Gomes, managing director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Republic National Bank of New York. "Plus we've had some whippy markets which have left people confused and not in any rush to do anything." \par \par \uc2  The dollar's downdrift following Greenspan negated an early upswing sparked by news that Group of Seven deputy finance ministers would meet next week. \par \par \uc2  Although officials said the meetings would focus on general economic themes ahead of meetings to be held later in Washington, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders speculated U.S. and Japanese officials might discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action to quell the yen's recent sharp appreciation against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "Anytime you get these guys around a table and dollar/yen is around 110, the market will assume there will talk of concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Jamie Coleman, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Thomson Global Markets. \par \par \uc2  The possibility of fresh Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to tame the yen's strength - with or without the help of the U.S. monetary authorities - remains on the market's mind. \par \par \uc2  A strong performance in recent months by the Japanese stock market and hedging against \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk by Japanese institutional investors have both strongly boosted the yen against the greenback. \par \par \uc2  The BoJ has repeatedly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rein in the yen since June, fearful that a strong yen could jeopardise Japan's economic recovery, but it has not been seen in the market for several weeks. \par \par \uc2  In other trade, the British pound firmed to $1.5882/92, from $1.5865/75, while the dollar edged down agains the Swiss franc to 1.5303/13 francs from 1.5330/40 francs previously. ((-North American Treasury Desk +1 212 859 1868)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv8r01r42\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
