\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar dented by Dow's declines after jittery week. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Elizabeth Lazarowitz \par \uc2 728 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 August 1999\par 00:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Aug 27 (Reuters) - After being whipsawed by a week of often volatile trading, the exhausted dollar spent most of Friday's session in hibernation, ending the day barely softer against the Japanese yen and Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Disappointing performance in U.S. stock and bond markets pulled the dollar lower late in the New York session, although it failed to shake the greenback from tight Friday ranges. \par \par \uc2  "Everyone was looking to see what the Dow would do...and now that the Dow's heavy the dollar's just a little heavy, too," said Vincent Amaru, vice president at Citibank. \par \par \uc2  The Dow ended more than 100 points lower at 11,090, while the benchmark 30-year bond stood more than a full point lower to yield 5.97 percent. \par \par \uc2  But the dollar remained more than one yen above a fresh seven-month low scraped earlier in the week near 110.36. \par \par \uc2  In late U.S. trading, the dollar slipped to 111.35/45 after heading into New York dealing at 111.53/63, barely changed from 111.50/60 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar also lost ground against the euro, which crept to $1.0462/69 from $1.0448/55 in early U.S. trading and from $1.0444/51 as Thursday's session wore down. \par \par \uc2  Comments from Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan that the Fed must watch asset prices in determining monetary policy, although he avoided commenting directly on the value of U.S. stocks, spooked Wall Street and hurt the dollar, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Reading between the lines...Greenspan seems to be indicating that the U.S. market is overvalued and traders are going to assume that the dollar could come under pressure if stocks sell off," said David Gilmore, partner at \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics. \par \par \uc2  The Fed earlier this week raised two key short-term interest rates by a quarter point and maintained its neutral monetary policy bias, which markets have interpreted to mean there would be no more rate rises this year. \par \par \uc2  But some dealers said the market would be eyeing key U.S. employment data next week for further confirmation. \par \par \uc2  "If the data come in strong and tightening speculation does begin to pick up and it creates a bit of a problem for bonds and stocks, the dollar probably weakens as a result," said Robert Lynch, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Banque Paribas \par \par \uc2  Trading conditions were extremely thin Friday, with London markets headed into a long holiday weekend. \par \par \uc2  But the market remained wary as talk swirled through the markets about the potential for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rein in the yen's rise. \par \par \uc2  The yen held the upper hand for much of the week, wrestling the euro to lifetime lows just 115.50 and denting the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Market jitters had lifted the dollar slightly on news that Group of Seven deputy finance ministers would meet next week. \par \par \uc2  Although officials said the meetings would focus on general economic themes ahead of meetings to be held later in Washington, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders speculated that U.S. and Japanese officials might discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action to quell the yen's recent sharp appreciation against the dollar. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  concerns were highlighted later Friday when Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  deputy governor official Yutaka Yamaguchi said Japan would obviously be worried if the yen appreciates faster than economic activity and asset markets improve. \par \par \uc2  But many market analysts were sceptical that BoJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is forthcoming, particularly if Japanese asset markets continue to thrive. \par \par \uc2  "They probably want to avoid premature strength in the yen, but it does not appear at this stage...they're ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  any time soon," Banque Paribas' Lynch said. \par \par \uc2  In other trading, the dollar slipped against the Swiss franc to 1.5285/95 francs from 1.5313/23 as the New York session kicked in and from 1.5330/40 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The greenback lost more than a full Canadian cent against the Canadian dollar, which attracted steady buying as gold and copper, commodities key to the Canadian economy, rebounded. The dollar dropped to C$1.4887/97 from C$1.5010/20 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The British pound was barely changed at $1.5870/80 from $1.5865/75 as Thursday's session wound down. \par \par \uc2  ((N.A. Treasury Desk, 212-859-1607)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv8r01nuf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
