\par\par \b \uc2  Japan Watch: The Legacy Of Mr Yen Haunts MOF \b0\par\par\uc2  By Takeshi Takeuchi \par \uc2 786 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 August 1999\par 02:02\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Just a month after the retirement of Eisuke Sakakibara, Japan's high-profile \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  czar, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  is grappling with the legacy of his dynamic style of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  management - and finding itself partially handcuffed, analysts and officials say. \par \par \uc2  In his glory days, the former vice finance minister for international affairs was a man to be reckoned with, bullying and cajoling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to make them reflect his views on economic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  Famous for his tirades against what he called "market fundamentalists," Sakakibara earned himself a name - "Mr. Yen" - by taking on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders when he felt they were out of control. He backed up his warnings to the market with heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , buying or selling the yen depending on which side of the equation needed the helping hand. \par \par \uc2  Most recently, Sakakibara made his last stand to defend dollar support at Y120. Monetary officials close to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy say that Sakakibara ordered his lieutenants to "to keep the dollar above Y120 by all means" to safeguard Japan's economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  In that effort, the Bank of Japan purchased some $20 billion to weaken the yen in June. In so doing, Japanese monetary officials managed to convince Japanese investors that Y120 would hold. \par \par \uc2  But exit Sakakibara in early July. And that confidence in Sakakibara's influence soon came back to haunt the ministry as the dollar fell Y120 level and Japanese offiblem: Japanese corporate traders who were convinced that Sakakibara would succeed in keeping the dollar above Y120 failed to hedge their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  exposure sufficiently. As the dollar has fallen from the low Y120s to current levels around Y111.50, many of these accounts have become increasingly eager to sell dollars on any blip higher. \par \par \uc2  And that makes it even more difficult for the ministry to ask the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to push ahead with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  now. With so much Japanese interest to sell dollars now, any rally sparked by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be quickly buried by selling pressure from these positions. \par \par \uc2  "We don't want to take decisive action (in the form of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) if it won't have a good effect," another monetary official close to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy told Dow Jones on condition of anonymity. "The current market situation doesn't seem very optimal (for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 )," he added. \par \par \uc2  Many market players seems to agree. \par \par \uc2  "Japanese exporters and institutional investors still have huge amounts of dollars between Y115 and Y120 unhedged because they didn't think it necessary to hedge," says Ryuichi Takami, vice president of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Sanwa Bank. "Right now, BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would only be absorbed by those accounts and wouldn't have any substantial effect on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels." \par \par \uc2  Some players appear to be growing desperate. Wednesday morning, minutes after top finance ministry officials issued standard warnings about an overly strong yen, the dollar shot up more than a yen in what many traders first mistook for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Later, participants traced the action to a Japanese institutional investor that may have been trying to trigger a dollar rally by stoking \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  concerns. \par \par \uc2  Many people in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's private and public sectors would like to see the dollar rise back toward Y120. There's a very real sense that the strong yen could snuff out the economic recovery before it catches fire. \par \par \uc2  According to Makoto Ishikawa, economist at Japan Research Institute, if the dollar stays around Y110 over the coming year, it will reduce Japan's gross domestic product by 0.2%. \par \par \uc2  "Exporters' profits and capital expenditures will be hurt by the yen's appreciation. The yen's rise at this stage is certainly undesirable for the Japanese economy," Ishikawa said. \par \par \uc2  And yet there seems to be little Japan can do to rein in the yen. Last week, BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami basically told Japanese companies to get used to the strong yen. \par \par \uc2  "Authorities can't do something each time (there is a change in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 )," Hayami said in a press conference. He added that Japanese companies "should strive to think of ways to make their management not affected by (changes in) \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels." \par \par \uc2  This wouldn't be the case if Sakakibara were still around, many say. On the other hand, it probably wouldn't be the case if Sakakibara had never taken office in the first place, others say. \par \par \uc2  "Those last \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  have now turned into nothing but a negative legacy of Mr. Sakakibara's. Japanese monetary authorities cannot easily buy dollars now because of his \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Yasunari Ueno, chief economist at Fuji Securities. \par \par \uc2  -By Takeshi Takeuchi (813-5255-2929;ttakeuchi@ap.org) \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 26-08-99 \par \par \uc2  0002GMT \par \par \uc2  1187 4256 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv8q0690z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
