\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar paces in ranges in early New York trade. \b0\par\par\uc2 469 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 August 1999\par 17:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Aug 26 (Reuters) - The dollar stayed within recently trod territory against the Japanese yen and European single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in early New York trade on Thursday as the market searched in vain for direction-giving news. \par \par \uc2  The market shrugged off data showing U.S. economic growth had slowed to its lowest level in a year in the second quarter, in line with economists' forecasts. \par \par \uc2  Gross domestic product expanded at a revised 1.8 percent annual rate, less than half the first quarter's 4.3 percent advance. \par \par \uc2  "The GDP didn't have any effect whatsoever - it was as expected," said Dennis Heidt, chief dealer at Banque Paribas. "This week we've already had news with much greater impact, such as the Fed rate move and gyrations in the stock market." \par \par \uc2  The Federal Reserve's quarter-point rate hike of two key short-term interest rates on Tuesday has strengthened the dollar's position against the euro and other European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the start of the week, he added. \par \par \uc2  Widening interest rate differentials between the U.S. and Europe should help boost the dollar. Earlier the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left its key refinancing rate unchanged at 2.50 percent as expected, spurring a modest downtick in the euro. \par \par \uc2  Still, the euro was firmer against the dollar as U.S. trade got underway, reflecting its overnight short-covering rebound from lows just above the $1.04 level. The euro also managed to stay away from lifetime lows hit against the yen overnight below 115.50 yen. \par \par \uc2  The euro traded at $1.0446/53 at 0830 EDT/1230 GMT, from $1.0418/23 in late U.S. trade on Wednesday, but subsequently slipped back after upwards momentum dissipated. \par \par \uc2  The dollar strode higher against the yen as fresh concerns about possible Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unnerved the markets. Traders said rate moves were also exaggerated by thin trading conditions. \par \par \uc2  After trading at 111.22/32 as New York dealing got underway, the dollar pushed higher to touch a session high of 111.85 yen, up a full yen from lows hit overnight. \par \par \uc2  "As the weekend approaches and trade thins out, no one wants to be short dollars, even though the instinct is to buy yen," said Mitsuaki Kataoka, vice president at Bank of Tokyo Mitsubishi. \par \par \uc2  The yen's appeal, traders say, reflects the strong performance of Japanese stocks and hedging of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk by Japanese institutional investors. \par \par \uc2  In other trade, the dollar slid against the British pound, which stood at $1.5890/00 as U.S. trade got underway compared to $1.5845/55. The greenback slipped to 1.5328/38 Swiss francs from 1.5373/83 francs. \par \par \uc2  ((-Lida Poletz, N.A. Treasury Desk +1 212 859 1868)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv8q01tbw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
