\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE-Dollar yield knocks down euro, pound. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Pierre Tran \par \uc2 1,241 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 August 1999\par 17:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 25 (Reuters) - The dollar climbed on Wednesday against most \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as investors welcomed the higher yield the U.S. Federal Reserve delivered with its quarter percentage point hike in interest rates in the previous session. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised the key fed funds and discount rates to 5.25 percent on Tuesday, as expected, and reassured markets by holding a neutral stance on future moves. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pushed up against the euro, Swiss franc and sterling and was only outgunned by the ascendant yen, as the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to enjoy broad-based strength on hopes of a domestic economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  "A higher interest rate means more yield, which is bullish for the dollar," said Glenn Davies, chief economist at Credit Lyonnais in London. \par \par \uc2  A rate rise raises the risk of a collapse in asset prices, as borrowing costs for companies and consumers increase, but European bond and share markets took the widely expected U.S. tightening in their stride with barely a murmur. \par \par \uc2  Euro/dollar fell, with the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading near five-week lows hit on Tuesday. The euro traded at $1.0452/62 at 1504 GMT compared with $1.0494/96 late on Tuesday in Europe. \par \par \uc2  Over the years the markets have come to respect the Fed for finely judging its call on U.S. rates in response to domestic and international economic conditions. The latest rate rise undoes two of the three rate cuts the Fed made last year. \par \par \uc2  But the head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales at a European bank in Paris said his corporate clients were not concerned at the euro's fall against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "The recovery in the euro to $1.08 seen a few weeks ago is not in doubt. The move to $1.04 from $1.08 is just a correction." \par \par \uc2  French companies are betting on a recovery in the euro, based on improved economic growth, which will allow them to buy dollars to finance mergers and acquisitions, he said. \par \par \uc2  "There are a lot of companies in Paris who need to buy dollars, to pay for purchases of U.S. companies, or other foreign companies and paying in dollars," he said. "They're waiting for $1.08 or more, they are not rushing to sell euros at $1.04." \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the rise in U.S. interest rates put pressure on sterling as it put U.S. rates above 5.0 percent British base rates, leaving holders of sterling at a disdavantage. \par \par \uc2  "The fact that fed funds are now above the UK's repo rate means that for the first time in more than a decade U.S. investors are penalised for holding money in UK deposits as opposed to U.S. ones," said Ryan Shea, economist at First Chicago in London. \par \par \uc2  The pound traded at $1.5884/94, after retracing from a four-week low at $1.5840 in the day and from $1.5970/80 on late Tuesday in Europe. \par \par \uc2  But the pound's fall was largely a knee-jerk reflex as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was supported by sound fundamentals with the economoy growing and inflation weak, Credit Lyonnais's Davies said. \par \par \uc2  "On balance, the UK looks pretty good. We might establish a new trading range but we do not regard this as a new trend," he said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose against the Swiss franc close to Tuesday's near-five-week highs, before easing to 1.5316/21 compared with 1.5254/64 late on Tuesday in Europe. \par \par \uc2  But the yen more than held its own against the dollar, with dollar/yen pushing down below 111 yen toward the important 110 level at which traders fear the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Japanese authorities have said they would act decisively to stop a premature rise in the yen as this would damage the competitiveness of exporters and chances of domestic recovery. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen was at 110.86/95 yen at 1512 GMT compared to the overnight seven-month low of 110.41 yen. \par \par \uc2  The euro fell to a life low against the yen for the second consecutive day, dropping below 116 yen before recovering to 116.01/20, from 117.19/29 late on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  ((London Capital Markets +44 171 542 2737, pierre.tran@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  Over the years the markets have come to respect the Fed for finely judging its call on U.S. rates in response to domestic and international economic conditions. The latest rate rise undoes two of the three rate cuts the Fed made last year. \par \par \uc2  But the head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales at a European bank in Paris said his corporate clients were not concerned at the euro's fall against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "The recovery in the euro to $1.08 seen a few weeks ago is not in doubt. The move to $1.04 from $1.08 is just a correction." \par \par \uc2  French companies are betting on a recovery in the euro, based on improved economic growth, which will allow them to buy dollars to finance mergers and acquisitions, he said. \par \par \uc2  "There are a lot of companies in Paris who need to buy dollars, to pay for purchases of U.S. companies, or other foreign companies and paying in dollars," he said. "They're waiting for $1.08 or more, they are not rushing to sell euros at $1.04." \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the rise in U.S. interest rates put pressure on sterling as 