\par\par \b \uc2  US- \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  joint  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  unlikely soon-Warburg. \b0\par\par\uc2 407 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 August 1999\par 19:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Aug 25 (Reuters) - The yen's recent rise against the dollar may have unnerved officials in both Tokyo and Washington, but competing interests mean joint U.S.-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to tame the yen is unlikely, Warburg Dillon Read said in a report. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has lost some 10 percent of its value against the yen since early July, stirring fears that the United States and Japan might coordinate to rein in the yen. \par \par \uc2  But while Japanese and U.S. monetary authorities might like to clip the yen's wings, Japan will probably have to go it alone to keep the yen from soaring further, the report said. \par \par \uc2  The four key players in any joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - the Federal Reserve, the U.S. Treasury, the Bank of Japan and Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  - have competing interests that will likely keep the United States and Japan from seeing eye-to-eye on the issue. \par \par \uc2  The Fed would be reluctant to participate in a joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for fear that targeting a specific exchange rate could put financial market stability at risk, the report said. \par \par \uc2  The Fed may also be unwilling to fight the dollar's downtrend, which some see as inevitable because of the burgeoning U.S. current account deficit, and remains fearful a coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  might fail anyway. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. Treasury, meanwhile, faces political pressures from U.S. producers that make it hard to back a stronger dollar. \par \par \uc2  The Treasury would also want \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to leave any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "unsterilized" in order to raise the probability of success, the report said. In an unsterilized \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  lets \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases or sales change the amount of money in circulation, thus changing monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  But the BoJ would likely prefer to keep a firm grip on monetary policy rather than cede control to MoF bureaucrats. \par \par \uc2  In addition, the BoJ might argue signs of life have already begun to appear in Japan's economy, helped by near-zero short-term interest rates and excess liquidity in the banking system, making unsterilized \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  superfluous at best. \par \par \uc2  With a joint U.S.-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  effort out of the picture for now, the dollar will be left to struggle under the weight of Japan's improving economic outlook and the yawning U.S. current account deficit, the report said. \par \par \uc2  ((Elizabeth Lazarowitz, N.A. Treasury Desk, 212-859-1607)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv8p01vkz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
