\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Japan MITI index shows slow, steady recovery. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Reed Stevenson \par \uc2 649 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 August 1999\par 05:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Aug 25 (Reuters) - Japan's economy remained on course for a slow but steady recovery in the April-to-June quarter, although it may need more government stimulus to pull out of a decade of stagnation, Trade Ministry data showed on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Economists said a slight 0.4 percent contraction in the ministry's overall business activity index, which approximates Japan's key gross domestic product data, was strong enough to show the economy was holding steady but weak enough to justify more spending by the government to push it along. \par \par \uc2  "Even though the number was negative, it shows that the economy is keeping the same level and it's not changing direction at this stage," Hiroyuki Inoue, senior economist at the Japan Center for Economic Research, told Reuters Television. \par \par \uc2  "It's difficult to tell when we (will) completely hit bottom," Inoue said, since the economy was still being supported by government spending. \par \par \uc2  Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi said on Wednesday that an extraordinary session of parliament should be held this fall in order to discuss another stimulative budget for the current fiscal year to next March. \par \par \uc2  Government officials have already indicated that another spending package was necessary to maintain a floor for the economy, and Obuchi said on Wednesday this was a "viable idea". \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, separately released trade data on Wednesday showed that exports continued to offer support for the economy. \par \par \uc2  Japan's politically sensitive trade surplus with the United States rose eight percent in July from a year earlier to 721.3 billion yen, although the overall trade surplus shrank 4.6 percent in July, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said. \par \par \uc2  The trade data and the Trade Ministry's index had little impact on Japanese bonds and the dollar/yen rate, both of which retained their bearish tone on Wednesday amid perceptions that Japan's economic outlook is improving. \par \par \uc2  The dollar tumbled to a seven-month low of 110.40 yen in early Tokyo trade, although it briefly spiked upward to 112.40 yen amid short-lived rumours of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  before settling back into its overnight trading range. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was quoted around 111 yen at 0300 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Haruhiko Kuroda on Wednesday reiterated Japan's stance that a premature strengthening of the yen was undesirable. \par \par \uc2  "Our \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is absolutely unchanged," Kuroda told reporters. "We are ready to take decisive action against a premature yen appreciation." \par \par \uc2  For some, the business index was a precursor to further yen strength and a respectable performance in April-to-June GDP, which has been widely expected to pull back after a stunning two percent quarter-on-quarter surge in the January-to-March period. \par \par \uc2  "The (April-June business index) results are strong. They came in the higher end of earlier expectations among many analysts," said Mitsumaru Kumagai, senior market analyst at the Industrial Bank of Japan. \par \par \uc2  "This will be a positive factor for the yen," he said. \par \par \uc2  The Trade Ministry said the overall business activity index for June dipped 0.1 percent, which was better than the 0.4 percent contraction called for in the average of economists' forecasts in a Reuters survey. \par \par \uc2  The tertiary services index, a major component of the overall figure, was unchanged in June from the previous month. \par \par \uc2  The ministry made downward revisions, however, in the overall index for April and May, although it said the data as a whole pointed to an economic uptrend. \par \par \uc2  "The decline was an adjustment to strong growth in the previous quarter. The trend (in the index) is still improving," a ministry official told reporters. He added that the ministry hoped the index would recover in the July-to-September quarter. ((Tokyo newsroom, +813-3432-9052 FAX +813-3432-8966 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv8p01o9o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
