\par\par \b \uc2  OPTIONS -  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  vols fall back after U.S. rate hike. \b0\par\par\uc2 546 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 August 1999\par 00:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  CHICAGO, Aug 24 (Reuters) - Front-end \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options volatilities softened Tuesday while the dollar gyrated up and back along with U.S. stocks after the Federal Reserve boosted key U.S. interest rates by a quarter-point. \par \par \uc2  One-month euro/dollar volatility was finishing the U.S. day at 10.35/10.55 percent, a touch firmer than where it ended Monday, after being offered as high as 10.8 percent before the Fed raised short-term interest rates in the early afternoon, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen volatility, meanwhile, was paid as high as 14.8 percent in one-month options when the dollar tested the day's low of 110.70 yen following the Fed announcement. As the dollar steadied near 111 yen, volatility subsequently cooled to 14.5/14.7 percent, still above Monday's 14.3/14.6 percent. \par \par \uc2  The dollar first rose, then fell and remained soft after the Fed news, its choppy path dictated by twists in U.S. stock prices. The Dow Jones Industrial Average ended the day down 16.46 points at 11,283.30 after changing course several times. Bonds rallied, with the 30-year Treasury climbing 23/32. \par \par \uc2  "Euro vols really depend on spot action in the next 48 hours. There is a real sense of illiquidity, and if that continues, you'll see the curve steepen," said an options trader for a European bank in New York. \par \par \uc2  Some traders and analysts argued the widening interest rate differentials, the ability of stocks to hold their ground despite higher U.S. rates and expected increased flows into U.S. asset markets before year-end meant the dollar ultimately should strengthen versus the euro. \par \par \uc2  "Equity markets have held up pretty well, so longer-term the euro is going to take a beating against the dollar," said MaryBeth Slack, senior options analyst for Thomson Global Markets. \par \par \uc2  She added some investors were paying up for overnight dollar/yen options amid jitters that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could decide to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen now that the Fed meeting is out of the way. \par \par \uc2  Saying its actions aimed to prevent a rise in inflation, the Fed lifted the federal funds rate for overnight interbank lending to 5.25 percent and also raised the discount rate for direct borrowing from the Fed by 25 basis points to 4.75 percent. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also said it would adopt a neutral stance toward interest rates later on, as it did after raising the fed funds rate a quarter point in June. \par \par \uc2  In a Reuters poll Tuesday, 25 of 30 U.S. primary dealers said they did not expect the Fed to raise rates again before year-end. \par \par \uc2  Volatility in one-year euro/dollar options also finished a little higher at 9.9/10.1 percent, while one-year dollar/yen was weaker at 12.85/13.0 percent. \par \par \uc2  Risk reversals were holding a slight euro-bullish bias of 0.2/0.4 in favour of the euro calls across the curve and a strong yen-upside slant of 2.5/2.8 for the yen calls. \par \par \uc2  ((Susan Kelly, Chicago Derivatives Desk 312-408-8750 chicago.derivatives.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv8o01x7f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
