\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Japan insists it not learning to love high yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By William Mallard \par \uc2 791 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 August 1999\par 10:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Aug 23 (Reuters) - Japanese officials sought on Monday to temper the impression that they are learning to love the strong yen, renewing threats of action to curb its rise. Senior \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials reiterated their concern about the yen, playing down comments on Sunday by their boss, Kiichi Miyazawa, that the yen's rise reflected a welcome "buy Japan" mentality. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa's remark in a rare television interview from his vacation spot outside Tokyo "was a comment made within the context that there is no change in our fundamental stance regarding \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 ", said Vice Minister Nobuaki Usui. \par \par \uc2  The ministry has said for months it was ready to take "decisive action" to block a premature rise in the yen from derailing Japan's nascent economic recovery, although it has not been seen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market since July 21. \par \par \uc2  The authorities had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily, hoisting the dollar briefly above 122 yen, after brighter economic data and business sentiment began pushing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  upward in early June. \par \par \uc2  But they have since stood by as the yen strengthened, offering only verbal warnings that have been increasingly discounted by the market. \par \par \uc2  The yen's rise accelerated last week, with the dollar sliding below 111 yen, as Tokyo shares cleared the 18,000 mark on the benchmark Nikkei 225 stock average for the first time in a month. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa, who said he had shunned television appearances in his 13 months as finance minister due to uncertainties about the economy, told a Sunday talk show that he finally thought things would turn out all right. \par \par \uc2  He made his most explicit promise yet of a supplementary budget to ensure that the recovery stays on track and cast the yen's climb in a favourable light. \par \par \uc2  "Rather than a speculative movement, it is the buying of Japan's future," Miyazawa said on a Fuji Television programme. \par \par \uc2  This comment, in particular, contrasted with a remark on Friday by the ministry's top diplomat Haruhiko Kuroda that recent yen-buying appeared to be driven by speculation. \par \par \uc2  When a panellist reminded Miyazawa that his remarks could well affect \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading on Monday, he quickly added: "Since Japan's economy will not improve that quickly, we are going to take action if the yen moves in a hasty way." \par \par \uc2  Indeed, the yen continued to gain ground on Monday as the market saw yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as less likely and as the Nikkei continued to rise. The dollar slipped to 110.65 yen in late afternoon trading, down about three-quarters of a yen from late on Friday in New York and its lowest since January 12. \par \par \uc2  As it turned out, the markets largely shrugged off Miyazawa's comments, but his deputies were nonetheless quick to stress that their policy against a premature yen rise was in effect. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda, the vice minister for international affairs, dropped his reference to a speculative cause for the yen's rise in a regular morning ambush by reporters on his way into his office. \par \par \uc2  But he stressed the ministry's long-held view: "An excessive and premature rise in the yen is not favourable for the Japanese and world economies." Stock rises reflected hopes of a recovery, but \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  values should reflect the fundamentals at any given time, he said. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda has sought in recent days to draw a distinction between higher Tokyo share prices, which he welcomes, and the higher yen, saying stocks could rise and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  stay stable at the same time. \par \par \uc2  "Foreign investors may be buying Japanese stocks but they don't necessarily have to take a position in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to do so," Kuroda said, adding that they could borrow yen or hedge their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions when they buy Japanese stocks. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda's deputy, Zembei Mizoguchi, head of the ministry's International Bureau, told reporters: "We will be watching movements in the market carefully and will take action." \par \par \uc2  The markets have been on edge for weeks, guessing when such action might come, and whether it might involve cooperation from the U.S. authorities - without which many market players believe \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be ineffective. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers earlier iced expectations of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by urging \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to boost domestic demand to generate a recovery from its worst postwar recession, rather than "manipulate" its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchange rate. \par \par \uc2  Speculation focuses on the 110 yen level for the dollar, below which the U.S. Federal Reserve has bought dollars in the past, with participants keenly awaiting the outcome of a Federal Open Market Committee monetary policy meeting starting on Tuesday. ((William Mallard, Tokyo newsroom +81-3 3432-8022 email: tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv8n022jc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pich