\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar trims losses against euro, yen in US trade. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Elizabeth Lazarowitz \par \uc2 661 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 August 1999\par 20:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Aug 20 (Reuters) - The dollar found its footing against both Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Japanese yen in midday U.S. trade on Friday, taking a breather after Thursday's sharp slide, as U.S. stock and bond markets perked up. \par \par \uc2  "The Dow has staged a nice little bounce and the long end of the bond market is doing a little better as well," said Jamie Coleman, managing \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Thomson Global Financial Markets. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the better stock and bond prices were giving the dollar a chance to collect itself after losing two cents against the euro on Thursday and falling more than four percent versus the yen in the past week. \par \par \uc2  "You're seeing a little bit a short-covering of dollar positions as you go into the weekend," Coleman added. \par \par \uc2  At noon, the euro stood barely higher against the dollar at $1.0648/55 from $1.0645/50 at Thursday's close, after climbing to fresh one week highs in early U.S. trade. \par \par \uc2  But it had lost considerable ground from $1.0683/90 as the U.S. session kicked in after it ran into technical resistance at one-week highs not far below $1.07. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market largely shrugged off comments from Bundesbank directorate member Edgar Meister, who said he did not see a need for a change in European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  monetary policy in the short term. Meister made the comments in a German newspaper released to Reuters in advance. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the dollar was slightly weaker against the yen at 111.33/43 at the New York session's midday from 111.42/52 as U.S. trading began and from 111.60/70 at Thursday's U.S. close. \par \par \uc2  But the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had managed to recover somewhat after it dipped as low as 111.05 yen in early U.S. trading and threatened to test Thursday's seven-month low. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the Bank of Japan's recent lack of effort to crimp the yen's sharp rise, which is feared would stifle Japan's nascent recovery, has encouraged market players to buy yen. \par \par \uc2  "As long as the Nikkei stays firm, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and BoJ really are not worried about the yen level," said Vincent Palazzolo, chief dealer at Sumitomo Bank New York. \par \par \uc2  Overnight, Japan's key Nikkei-225 stock index rose 1.22 percent. \par \par \uc2  While fresh concerns about potential \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rein in the yen had emerged in Tokyo trading hours, traders said the dollar is likely due for more declines. \par \par \uc2  News that Bank of Japan deputy Governor Yutaka Yamaguchi is due to visit the United States for 10 days from Saturday sparked market speculation of monetary or \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy coordination between Japan and the United States. \par \par \uc2  "We could get a short-term pop (in the dollar against the yen), but I still believe over the next few weeks the trend here is clearly going to be down," said Jay Bryson, international economist at First Union National Bank. \par \par \uc2  But U.S. traders were sceptical that a coordinated U.S.-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in the cards near-term, especially ahead of the Federal Reserve's Federal Open Market Committee meeting scheduled for Tuesday, August 24. \par \par \uc2  In other trade, the dollar was slightly lower against the Swiss franc at 1.4980/90 against 1.5000/10 at the previous close, but had recovered from 1.4942/52 at the open. \par \par \uc2  It was slightly weaker against the Canadian dollar at C$1.4937/47 from C$1.4952/62 at the open and C$1.4945/55 at the end of the previous session. \par \par \uc2  The British pound slipped to $1.6142/52 after starting U.S. trade at $1.6192/02, up from from $1.6170/80 at the previous close. \par \par \uc2  ((North American Treasury Desk +1 212 859 1607)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv8k0285o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
