\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE- \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  ghost lurks at yen party. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Carolyn Cohn \par \uc2 954 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 August 1999\par 17:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 20 (Reuters) - The yen hung back from the previous day's seven-month peaks against the dollar and all-time highs against the euro in Europe on Friday, amid the threat of further Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb yen strength. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  at least seven times in June and July to stop the runaway yen, but was last detected in the market selling yen on July 21. \par \par \uc2  The yen has rallied from a low of 119.66 per dollar on that date to a high of 110.65 on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  But it was trapped above 111 per dollar on Friday, after the Bank of Japan said its deputy governor, Yutaka Yamaguchi, would visit the United States for 10 days from Saturday, raising the spectre of concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  between the BOJ and the U.S. Federal Reserve. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was trading at 111.23/26 at 1430 GMT, from 111.10/13 in late European trade on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar and euro both managed to recover against the yen in pre-weekend book-squaring, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Euro/yen was trading at 118.52/56, two yen above Thursday's life low and from 118.24/28 in late Europe on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  But some analysts were sceptical that joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would take place, allowing the yen to strengthen further. \par \par \uc2  "The U.S. would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  only if dollar/yen weakened so much as to undermine Japan's recovery," said Tony Norfield, head of global treasury research at ABN AMRO in London. \par \par \uc2  "However at the moment they (the U.S.) are more concerned with what is going on domestically." \par \par \uc2  The U.S. Federal Open Market Committee meets next week, with a 25 basis point rise in U.S. interest rates widely expected. \par \par \uc2  Ichizo Ohara, a senior member of Japan's ruling Liberal Democratic Party, said on Friday that a dollar drop below 110 yen would be problematic, reinforcing the view that 110 was the level which could trigger \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if breached. \par \par \uc2  But Kirit Shah, chief strategist at Sanwa, said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unlikely with the dollar at anything above 105 yen. \par \par \uc2  Shah said he had detected a sea-change since Haruhiko Kuroda took over as Japan's top financial diplomat from "Mr Yen" Eisuke Sakakibara on July 9. \par \par \uc2  "The authorities are trying to send a message to the domestic market place that they should not expect support from the government, and should adjust to the movements of the market." \par \par \uc2  Shah said continued capital flows into Japanese assets would favour further yen gains. \par \par \uc2  The euro rallied more than two cents from the previous day's lows to one-week highs against the dollar on Friday, invigorated by a strong German Ifo survey and record U.S. trade deficit released on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  A more positive outlook on the euro zone, which has Germany as its largest economy, was reinforced by comments from Bundesbank president-designate Ernst Welteke. \par \par \uc2  Welteke said on Friday that the economic recovery in the euro zone and weakness of the U.S. trade balance should help the euro strengthen. \par \par \uc2  "The euro will continue to gain ground against the dollar," said Trevor Williams, senior economist at Lloyds in London. \par \par \uc2  "But the market needs more hard evidence than we have seen so far of a recovery, in Germany and Italy in particular." \par \par \uc2  Williams said the euro was likely to tackle $1.08 over the next week, rising to $1.10 before the end of the year. \par \par \uc2  The euro was trading at $1.0643/50 at 1500 GMT, from a one-week high on Friday of $1.0689 and from $1.0639/46 in late European trade on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The euro softened in late European trade after Bundesbank directorate member Edgar Meister said he did not see a need for the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to move interest rates in the short term. \par \par \uc2  A Reuters poll of 25 analysts released on August 11 showed five expected the ECB to raise rates in 1999 from the current 2.50 percent. \par \par \uc2  ((London Capital Markets +44 171 542 6320, carolyn.cohn@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  The euro rallied more than two cents from the previous day's lows to one-week highs against the dollar on Friday, invigorated by a strong German Ifo survey and record U.S. trade deficit released on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  A more positive outlook on the euro zone, which has Germany as its largest economy, was reinforced by comments from Bundesbank president-designate Ernst Welteke. \par \par \uc2  Welteke said on Friday that the economic recovery in the euro zone and weakness of the U.S. trade balance should help the euro strengthen. \par \par \uc2  "The euro will continue to gain ground against the dollar," said Trevor Williams, senior economist at Lloyds in London. \par \par \uc2  "But the market needs more hard evidence than we have seen so far of a recovery, in Germany and Italy in particular." \par \par \uc2  Williams said the euro was likely to tackle $1.08 over the next week, rising to $1.10 before the end of the year. \par \par \uc2  The euro was trading at $1.0643/50 at 1500 GMT, from a one-week high on Friday of $1.0689 and from $1.0639/46 in late European trade on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The euro softened in late European trade after Bundesbank directorate member Edgar Meister said he did not see a need for the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to move interest rates in the short term. \par \par \uc2  A Reuters poll of 25 analysts released on August 11 showed five expected the ECB to raise rates in 1999 from the current 2.50 percent. \par \par \uc2  ((London Capital Markets +44 171 542 6320, carolyn.cohn@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv8k027tz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
