\par\par \b \uc2  Euro, yen loom over dollar in U.S. trade. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Lida Poletz \par \uc2 533 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 August 1999\par 16:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Aug 20 (Reuters) - The European single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rode to new one-week highs against the dollar in early U.S. trade on Friday on the back of improving prospects for the euro zone economy. \par \par \uc2  The dollar also remained vulnerable to fresh declines against the Japanese yen after Thursday's decline to seven-month lows, although speculation about possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action to boost the dollar kept players on edge, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The euro's latest gains followed strength overnight and a two-cent rally on Thursday, triggered by a strong German business climate Ifo index and a record U.S. June trade deficit. \par \par \uc2  "If you look at the fundamentals, it's looking pretty good for the euro," said Brian Zirlin, vice president at Donaldson, Lufkin & Jenrette. "We're seeing a lot of euro/dollar buying." \par \par \uc2  As U.S. trade got underway the euro stood at $1.0683/90, up from $1.0645/50 at the close of the previous session, although it later slipped. \par \par \uc2  The session high of $1.0689 was near the psychologically significant $1.07 level, above which automatic signals to sell dollars will likely be triggered, traders said. That could unleash new euro gains towards $1.0730 and then $1.08. \par \par \uc2  Bundesbank President Ernst Welteke added to the euro-positive mood, saying in a German monthly magazine that the economic recovery in the euro zone and the weakness of the U.S. trade balance should help the euro strengthen. \par \par \uc2  The yen, which on Thursday had pounded the dollar to a low of 110.72 yen and bullied the euro to lifetime lows, continued to assert itself although it was off its peaks. \par \par \uc2  The dollar traded at 111.42/52 yen at 0830 EDT/1230 GMT, down from 111.60/70, and then continued to slip closer to the 111 level. \par \par \uc2  "We got a recovery in dollar/yen from lows yesterday with people squaring up positions ahead of the weekend, but looking ahead I suspect we'll see a test of 110," said Frank Pusateri, vice president of corporate \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Mellon Bank in Boston. \par \par \uc2  Fresh concerns about potential \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to tame yen strength emerged in Tokyo trading hours. \par \par \uc2  News that Bank of Japan deputy Governor Yutaka Yamaguchi is due to visit the United States for 10 days from Saturday sparked market speculation of monetary or \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy coordination between Japan and the United States. \par \par \uc2  But U.S. traders were sceptical that a coordinated U.S.-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in the cards near term, especially ahead of the Federal Reserve's Open Market Committee meeting scheduled for August 24. \par \par \uc2  In other trade, the dollar slipped against the Swiss franc to 1.4974/84 francs, against 1.5000/10 at the previous close, in line with its movements against the euro. \par \par \uc2  The British pound reached a two-week high against the dollar but was unable to hold above $1.62 resistance. It had started U.S. trade at $1.6192/02, up from from $1.6170/80 at the previous close. \par \par \uc2  ((-Lida Poletz, North American Treasury Desk +1 212 859 1868)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv8k027om\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
