\par\par \b \uc2  INTERVIEW- \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  action not needed now- \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Liberals. \b0\par\par\uc2 338 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 August 1999\par 09:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Aug 20 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  need not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at present to curb the yen's rise, Yoshio Suzuki, deputy head of the Liberal Party, the junior partner in Japan's ruling coalition, said on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Suzuki, a former Bank of Japan official, also told Reuters he agreed with the rejection by the BOJ and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  of radical further monetary easing. \par \par \uc2  He said five to 10 trillion yen would be an appropriate scale for an expected supplementary budget to stimulate the still-weak economy. \par \par \uc2  Suzuki said that while \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was necessary in the case of a rapid rise in the yen, the dollar was recovering naturally from its drop below 111 yen. \par \par \uc2  "For the present, I don't think it's drastic," he said. "It's all right to wait and see a bit more." \par \par \uc2  As factors mitigating the damage of a high yen, Suzuki said, Japanese companies rely heavily on imported raw materials, they have shifted much of their production to Asia and elsewhere, and the yen's trade-weighted value is around its late 1980s levels. \par \par \uc2  "Manufacturers have the ability to cope with a strong yen," Suzuki said. "A gradual yen rise is not a problem." \par \par \uc2  Suzuki said it was wrong to think that the high yen constitutes a serious deflationary threat and, further, to think that "quantitative" monetary easing would be effective in combating deflation. \par \par \uc2  "The idea of a further quantitative easing misses the point," Suzuki said. "I am of the same opinion as the MOF and BOJ on this." \par \par \uc2  The BOJ Policy Board, which has already driven short-term interest rates essentially to zero, has repeatedly rejected by 8-1 votes proposals to quantify an increase in a key money supply component and to target a positive inflation rate. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa has repeatedly expressed support for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's zero interest rate policy. ((Yuko Yoshikawa, Tokyo newsroom +81-3 3432-8022 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv8k027cy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
