\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr reclaims Y112,market wary over BOJ's US visit. \b0\par\par\uc2 756 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 August 1999\par 06:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Aug 20 (Reuters) - The dollar rose to 112 yen by Tokyo midday on Friday amid renewed caution over possible U.S.-Japan policy coordination, recovering from overnight news of a shock widening in the U.S. trade gap. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was buoyed in late morning by the Bank of Japan's (BOJ) announcement that the bank's deputy governor Yutaka Yamaguchi will visit the United States from Saturday. \par \par \uc2  This fuelled speculation that monetary or \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy coordination between Japan and the United States will be discussed during his visit. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ eased credit last September after Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa, accompanied by Yamaguchi, went to San Franciso to meet U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin. \par \par \uc2  The dollar had risen to 111.98/01 yen as of 0340 GMT from 111.60/70 yen in late New York trade on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Traders here said the dollar dipped to a fresh seven-month low of 110.68 yen on the wake of the U.S. trade data. \par \par \uc2  During his 10-day visit, Yamaguchi will attend a symposium in Jackson Hole, Wyoming, sponsored by the Kansas City Federal Reserve, the BOJ said on Friday. But no other details of the schedule of his visit were available. \par \par \uc2  "Speculators bought up the dollar because of the timing of Yamaguchi's visit. The FOMC meeting will be held while he is there," said a Japanese bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. Federal Open Market Committee meeting is due on August 24, when a 25-basis-point rate hike is widely expected. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers argue that if the BOJ takes credit easing steps beyond its current zero interest rate policy after the FOMC meeting, that would further widen the interest rate gap between Japan and the United States and cause a higher dollar against the yen. \par \par \uc2  Earlier on Friday, buying by Japanese importers and pre-weekend short-covering by U.S. operators supported the dollar, as did Japanese retail demand for dollar deposits, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "We need to pause from this week's heavy dollar selloffs. Today is the day for pre-holiday position unwinding," said a U.S. bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  The dollar hit a recent high of 116.40 yen in New York last Friday, backed by rises in U.S. asset prices on benign U.S. wholesale price data. But since then, it has been knocked down by growing optimism about Japanese economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  Friday's comments by senior Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official Haruhiko Kuroda had limited impact on the market although what looks like a strengthening tone reminded some dealers of possible Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb yen rise. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not sold yen for dollars in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since it last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on July 21, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The Finance Ministry's Kuroda told a group of reporters on Friday that a recent rapid rise in the yen does not reflect economic fundamentals and such movements, resulting from speculative dealings, were undesirable. \par \par \uc2  Asked about the dollar's fall below 111 yen overnight, Kuroda told reporters: "As I have said, a premature rise in the yen is undesirable not only for the Japanese economy but also for the global economy. \par \par \uc2  "The yen's recent sharp rise has slightly deviated from (economic) fundamentals and such speculative movement is undesirable," Kuroda said. \par \par \uc2  A city bank dealer said Kuroda's blaming the yen's rise on speculative dealings looked like an excuse for the authorities to remind market participants of possible action to rein in the yen. \par \par \uc2  "The MOF must have known the recent gains in the yen were not just because of speculative buying, but reflected foreigners' investment in Japanese stocks and yen buying by Japanese life insurers hedging foreign assets," he said. \par \par \uc2  The Industrial Bank of Japan , Dai-Ichi Kangyo Bank  and Fuji Bank released a statement on the details of their alliance before a news briefing started at 1:30 p.m. (0430 GMT) \par \par \uc2  There was no immediate reaction in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  As of 0441 GMT, the euro was at 119.17/31 yen after quickly recovering from Thursday's lifetime low of 116.60 yen in Tokyo partly due to subdued activity exaggerating the rebound, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The euro closed at 118.88 yen in New York on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $1.0632/42, compared with $1.0645/50 in late New York on Thursday. ((Risa Maeda, Tokyo Treasury Desk, +81-3-3432-8570 tokyo.news room@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv8k026t5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
