\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar buffeted by yen in US, euro stages rebound. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Lida Poletz \par \uc2 709 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 August 1999\par 19:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Aug 19 (Reuters) - The dollar licked its wounds in choppy New York trade on Thursday after news of a record U.S. trade gap in June sparked a fierce mauling by the Japanese yen and European single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Bearish dollar sentiment and a notable absence of Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to check the yen's ascent has left the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  vulnerable to fresh attacks, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar, which fell to seven-month lows under 111 yen in early U.S. trade, has shed a full five yen this week as investors applaud Japan's strong stock market performance amid signs of economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  "People are definitely long dollar/yen and there are stop losses all the way down, with options related stops below 110 (yen)," said Dennis Heidt, chief dealer at Paribas. "All these things point to a heavy dollar/yen." \par \par \uc2  As New York trading got underway U.S. Commerce Department trade data had shown the U.S. trade deficit widening to a record $24.62 billion in June, piling more pressure on the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Investors also dumped dollars for euros, which had already won accolades from investors in European trade after a stronger-than-expected German confidence index. \par \par \uc2  "The U.S. (trade) deficit is rising so quickly that there is some concern over whether it's sustainable, and whether foreign investors will continue to willingly finance such a large and growing deficit," said Douglas Porter, senior economist at Nesbitt Burns, explaining the dollar's reaction to the number. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. trade imbalance with Japan surged to $6.28 billion in June, while the gap with China rose to $5.67 billion. Imports from Western Europe soared to record highs, widening the deficit to $4.94 billion, the highest since July 1998. \par \par \uc2  After the trade data, the dollar tumbled to a low of 110.72 yen before bouncing back. A 1300 EDT the dollar stood at 111.18/28 yen, unchanged from the start of U.S. trade but down sharply from 111.82/92 at Wednesday's U.S. close. \par \par \uc2  The euro, meanwhile, gained more than a cent to trade at $1.0624/31 after starting New York trade at $1.0512/19, little changed from the previous close of $1.0520/27. \par \par \uc2  The euro had started its upswing during European trade from lows near $1.0450, after the Ifo business climate index for western Germany bucked market speculation of a weak number. It rose to 93.6 in July, confirming the view that growth in Europe's largest economy will accelerate this year. \par \par \uc2  "The breakout in dollar/yen was not sustained, but in the euro it has been sustained, partly because the euro has been weakish versus the dollar in the last week or so," said Diego Giurleo, vice president of Treasury at Royal Bank of Canada. \par \par \uc2  Market jitters eased regarding possible Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the dollar against the yen, but traders said they would be warier closer to the psychologically significant 110 yen level. \par \par \uc2  Japanese monetary authorities, fearing a rapid rise in the yen will choke off nascent economic recovery, have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  7 times since June 10 to tone down the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength. \par \par \uc2  In other trade, the British pound firmed alongside the euro to $1.6168.78 per pound, after starting at $1.6043/53, up from $1.6020/30 at the previous close. \par \par \uc2  The dollar slumped against the Swiss franc to 1.5038/48 francs, compared to 1.521/20 francs at the start of U.S. trade and 1.5204/14 francs at the previous close. \par \par \uc2  But the dollar rallied strongly against the Canadian dollar, hitting one-week highs of $1.4929/39 in midday trade, compared to from C$1.4866/76 at the start and C$1.4835/45 at the previous close. \par \par \uc2  Trade in the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was jittery ahead of the U.S. Federal Reserve's rate-setting meeting next week, which is expected to raise the key federal funds rate by 25 basis points. Higher interest rates in the United States could lure investments away from Canada and toward its southern neighbour. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv8j025j4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
