\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Japan seen relaxed as yen bulldozes higher. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Risa Maeda \par \uc2 655 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 August 1999\par 11:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Aug 19 (Reuters) - Signs of economic recovery and robust Tokyo stocks have eased Japanese concerns about the strong yen, and the dollar may fall below 110 yen before official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  again becomes a real threat, analysts say. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think Japan will feel alarmed at the (dollar's) current level of just below 112 yen," said Koji Fukaya, chief analyst at the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and treasury division of Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said that with the yen's rise doing little tangible damage to corporate sentiment so far, Japanese authorities have relaxed their \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy which saw the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) step in aggressively to sell yen in recent months. \par \par \uc2  Instead, the BOJ may be ready to wait until its U.S. counterpart is sufficiently concerned about the dollar's weakness to mount joint market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The BOJ was nowhere to be seen on Thursday, even as the yen surged to a lifetime high of 116.85 against the euro and a seven-month high of 111.20 in Tokyo afternoon trade. \par \par \uc2  Japan's top financial diplomat Haruhiko Kuroda repeated the MOF mantra on Thursday that Japan will take appropriate and decisive action as needed on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But the market was more interested in his reference to a possible de-coupling between the yen and Japanese share prices. \par \par \uc2  Traders said this reflected the ministry's current aim of leaving it to the market to judge if the yen's level is in line with economic conditions. \par \par \uc2  Asked about a recent rise in Japan's stock prices, Kuroda, the vice finance minister for international affairs, said: "A rise in stock prices reflects expectations for economic recovery, and that is favourable." \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is responsible for Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, has been trying to persuade \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders that gains in Tokyo stocks on hopes for an economic recovery should not necessarily be reflected in yen strength. \par \par \uc2  "There seems a consensus between Japanese and U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  out of line with a nation's economic fundamentals cannot be effective," said Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi's Fukaya. \par \par \uc2  He added that any action by the MOF would be more a smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy time until market participants realise the yen had been overbought. \par \par \uc2  Tuesday's comments by Bank of Japan (BOJ) Governor Masaru Hayami also fuelled the view that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy has become more market-oriented. \par \par \uc2  Hayami said in a news conference that the key issue regarding the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets is what corporations do to deal with \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuations, rather than what actions the authorities should take in response to short-term \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said U.S.-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to save the beleaguered dollar is still possible if the dollar falls below 110 yen and causes unstability in U.S. asset markets. \par \par \uc2  "We should be aware that the authorities haven't said that they would leave the yen's rise unchecked," said Daisaku Ueno, senior economist at Nomura Research Institute. \par \par \uc2  Ueno said Japan could step up its action to a stage of a joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with others, adding that Tokyo has cleared the previous stage in July when European and U.S. authorities sold the yen for the sake of the BOJ, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  conductor. \par \par \uc2  Washington has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a level above 110 yen since 1990, but U.S.-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is possible if the dollar falls below 110 yen, he said. \par \par \uc2  Former BOJ executive director Mikio Wakatsuki also said he saw U.S.-Japan joint action as possible if Europe's economic recovery starts to attract more capital to that region. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think U.S. authorities could maintain their indifference," Wakatsuki told Reuters Television. ((Risa Maeda, Tokyo Treasury Desk, +81-3-3432-8570 tokyo.news room@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv8j0256h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
