\par\par \b \uc2  Markets say Japan's Kuroda no match for Mr Yen yet. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Swaha Pattanaik \par \uc2 581 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 August 1999\par 13:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 19 (Reuters) - A job title carries less clout with the foreign exchanges than a proven track record of being able to move markets, as Japanese Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Haruhiko Kuroda is rapidly discovering. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda took over in July from Eisuke Sakakibara, dubbed "Mr. Yen" for his influence over \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but has found his thinly veiled threats of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb yen strength carry less weight than even rumours of what his predecessor said. \par \par \uc2  Repeated warnings by Kuroda that Japan will take action to prevent yen strength from derailing Japan's recovery have not prevented the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from hitting seven-month peaks against the dollar and all-time highs against the euro on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Particularly galling for Kuroda may be that the yen rally was fuelled on Wednesday by rumours, later denied, that Sakakibara had said Japan would tolerate dollar/yen losses to 110 as long as its share prices kept rising. \par \par \uc2  "It is strange that the guy who is holding the office is being ignored while the guy who left is the one people are taking note of, but a reputation is something that takes a while to build," said a senior trader at a U.S. bank in London. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara won the respect of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers for his part in engineering the yen's fall from the post-World-War-Two high it set against the dollar in 1995. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda has yet to demonstrate he can turn market sentiment around in the same way and will not win the respect of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers until he does, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The yen is nearly seven percent stronger against the dollar than it was before the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  launched the first of at least seven rounds of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on June 10. \par \par \uc2  "There was some sense that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would try to defend the 120 level in dollar/yen and now it seems to be 110, so who knows where it will be in a month's time," said Mark Cliffe, chief economist at ING Barings in London. \par \par \uc2  "Consistency is very important and if the market doesn't get it, it will just test the boundaries. At the moment, it looks like it is open season to 110 and if policymakers don't defend this level, they may end up having to defend 100." \par \par \uc2  If it is any comfort to Kuroda, he is not the only policymaker with the problem of having to live up to a more respected predecessor. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers is trying to fill the shoes of the highly influential Robert Rubin, who perfected the administration's strong dollar policy over the past three years. \par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , meantime, is still trying to emulate the credibility of the Bundesbank. \par \par \uc2  Even at the German \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Ernst Welteke is set to replace the tried and trusted Hans Tietmeyer as president at the end of this month. \par \par \uc2  "Kuroda, Summers, and the ECB are all trying to carve themselves in the image of their predecessors but the market is still suspicious," said Steve Barrow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Bear Stearns International in London. \par \par \uc2  "People are in no mood to give anyone the benefit of doubt and want to see something positive from new policymakers before they believe they are as good as the old policymakers" \par \par \uc2  ((London Capital Markets, +44 171 542 6284, swaha.pattanaik@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv8j020nv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
