\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Rising yen knocks down dollar, euro. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Pierre Tran \par \uc2 928 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 August 1999\par 13:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 18 (Reuters) - A surging yen sent the dollar reeling to six-month lows and pushed the euro to a lifetime trough on Wednesday with investor demand for Japanese stocks seen fuelling appetite for yen. \par \par \uc2  Hopes of a domestic recovery have boosted Japanese share prices, drawing strong flows into the yen. But traders are wary that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength could threaten \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recovery, and provoke \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the foreign exchanges. \par \par \uc2  "There's a desire to get into the yen but we are getting to levels which will be of concern to the authorities," said a trader at a European bank in London. \par \par \uc2  Euro/yen hit a life-low at 118.73 yen in Europe, down three percent so far this week, while dollar/yen fell as low as 112.78 yen, its weakest since February 8. That is down about 1.5 percent from Wednesday's highs and compares with 114.24/34 late on Tuesday in Europe. \par \par \uc2  "The yen's bashing everything," said Tony Norfield, head of global treasury research at ABN Amro in London. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Ministry official Haruhiko Kuroda said overnight the authorities would take decisive action to avoid a premature rise in the yen and repeated during the European session there had been no change in the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. \par \par \uc2  In jittery mid-morning trading, the dollar spiked up more than a 1/4 yen but traders said they saw no signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . There was also skepticism about whether solo Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be effective. \par \par \uc2  ABN Amro's Norfield said Japanese firms had left large orders to sell dollars around 115 and 116 yen and that this would hobble any post-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rally in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The yen's gains on Wednesday also took their toll on the euro but the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gained some relief in the morning as the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  suffered the brunt of the battering. \par \par \uc2  The European bank trader said the euro was unlikely to extend its gains too far against the dollar before the release of Germany's July Ifo report on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The strength of the June Ifo report provided solid foundations for the rally which saw the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rebound seven percent from life lows and hit three-month highs against the dollar earlier in August. \par \par \uc2  Euro/dollar was at $1.0523/28 at 1101 GMT, after hitting a day-high of $1.0550 and compared with three-and-a-half-week low of $1.0487 hit on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere in Europe, sterling fell from earlier four-week highs against the euro and softened against the dollar after minutes of the Bank of England's Monetary Policy Committee August meeting showed members were split over the need for further rate cuts. \par \par \uc2  "Some people thought there would be one or two MPC members looking for a rate rise," said a trader at a British bank. \par \par \uc2  The pound was further undermined by a report showing July retail sales rose a weaker-than-expected 0.1 percent compared with a forecast 0.5 percent rise. \par \par \uc2  Euro/sterling was at 65.63/69 pence at 1034 GMT, after rising to a day high of 65.71 pence after the MPC minutes and data, compared with 65.63/72 pence late on Tuesday in Europe. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was at the day's lows, trading at $1.6034/44 from Wednesday's peak of $1.6086. ((London Capital Markets +44 171 542 2737, pierre.tran@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  ABN Amro's Norfield said Japanese firms had left large orders to sell dollars around 115 and 116 yen and that this would hobble any post-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rally in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The yen's gains on Wednesday also took their toll on the euro but the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gained some relief in the morning as the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  suffered the brunt of the battering. \par \par \uc2  The European bank trader said the euro was unlikely to extend its gains too far against the dollar before the release of Germany's July Ifo report on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The strength of the June Ifo report provided solid foundations for the rally which saw the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rebound seven percent from life lows and hit three-month highs against the dollar earlier in August. \par \par \uc2  Euro/dollar was at $1.0523/28 at 1101 GMT, after hitting a day-high of $1.0550 and compared with three-and-a-half-week low of $1.0487 hit on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere in Europe, sterling fell from earlier four-week highs against the euro and softened against the dollar after minutes of the Bank of England's Monetary Policy Committee August meeting showed members were split over the need for further rate cuts. \par \par \uc2  "Some people thought there would be one or two MPC members looking for a rate rise," said a trader at a British bank. \par \par \uc2  The pound was further undermined by a report showing July retail sales rose a weaker-than-expected 0.1 percent compared with a forecast 0.5 percent rise. \par \par \uc2  Euro/sterling was at 65.63/69 pence at 1034 GMT, after rising to a day high of 65.71 pence after the MPC minutes and data, compared with 65.63/72 pence late on Tuesday in Europe. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was at the day's lows, trading at $1.6034/44 from Wednesday's peak of $1.6086. ((London Capital Markets +44 171 542 2737, pierre.tran@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv8i02e8e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
