\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Yen prowess cows euro and dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2 755 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 August 1999\par 10:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 18 (Reuters) - The yen was strong across the board early in Europe on Wednesday, rallying to six-month highs against the dollar and all-time peaks against the euro. \par \par \uc2  Expectations of a pick-up in Japanese economic activity is seen generating demand for Japanese stocks and the yen, while the recent absence of Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the foreign exchanges has encouraged bulls to keep pushing the yen higher. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Ministry official Haruhiko Kuroda reminded dealers overnight that the authorities were ready to take decisive action to prevent a premature yen rise which could hurt the country's economic recovery but analysts said verbal warnings would not be enough to stem yen gains. \par \par \uc2  "It will be difficult to stop the yen rising and words will certainly not be enough," said Ian Gunner, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN Amro in London. \par \par \uc2  "Now that dollar/yen has broken below 113.50, the risk is we could go down as far as 112 or even 111.50 by the end of the week." \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell to lows near 113.35 yen by 0659 GMT, its weakest since February 8, while the euro sank to troughs near 119 yen, down nearly three percent so far this week and more than nine percent weaker since its January launch. \par \par \uc2  These lows in the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  are equivalent to a mark/yen rate of about 60.85, the mark's weakest levels since July 1995. \par \par \uc2  Dealers have been more inclined to drive the yen higher since Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami said on Tuesday there were limits to what the authorities could do to combat persistent strength in the yen. He also urged firms to make greater efforts to shield themselves from \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swings. \par \par \uc2  "It looks like the Japanese know they can't do anything about this," said Jeff Woodruff, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of Boston in London. \par \par \uc2  "Given we don't see \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the cards, they are going to have to watch the yen go higher." \par \par \uc2  While the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  at least seven times since June 10 to curb yen strength, it has not been seen selling its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since July 21. \par \par \uc2  The yen initially made its biggest gains against the euro this week, driving the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a 3-1/2-week lows around $1.0485 on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  However, the sharp drop in dollar/yen seen early in Europe on Wednesday saw the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  begin to claw back ground. It was at $1.0541/45 from $1.0510/14 late in Europe on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Gloom about the dollar's prospects was compounded by its apparent inability to derive sustained support from gains U.S. stocks and bonds have racked up recently as U.S. economic reports have assuaged inflation fears. \par \par \uc2  "The market is disappointed by the dollar's performance overnight considering the rise in the Dow and the T-bonds," said Stephen Hannah, director of economic research at IBJ International in London. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's woes could soon be compounded if the closely-watched German July Ifo survey out on Thursday refocuses attention on a pick-up in economic activity in the 11-nation euro zone, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The strength of the June Ifo report provided solid foundations for the rally which saw the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rebound seven percent from life lows and hit three-month highs against the dollar earlier in August. \par \par \uc2  Expectations of healthier economic performance in the euro zone were reinforced after the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in its August monthly report released on Tuesday that there were now clear signs that the region's economy would speed up in the second half of 1999. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere in Europe, sterling was content to take its cue from moves in euro/dollar before the release of British July retail sales data and minutes of the Bank of England Monetary Policy Committee's August meeting at 0830 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Any signs of a pick up in economic activity or comments which reinforce the view that British rates have bottomed are expected to help the pound make independent gains. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was at $1.6076/86 at 0802 GMT, up 0.75 percent from the near-three-week lows it hit on Tuesday. Euro/sterling was at 65.49/55 pence from 65/63/72 late in Europe on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  ((Swaha Pattanaik, London Capital Markets, +44 171 542 6284, swaha.pattanaik@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv8i02acb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
