\par\par \b \uc2  Investors Embracing Yen Only Gingerly For Time Being \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chip Cummins \par \uc2 838 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 August 1999\par 16:03\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  LONDON (Dow Jones)--Despite the yen's record-breaking climb against the euro this week, talk of a major asset-allocation shift favoring the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be premature. \par \par \uc2  The yen hit a new, all-time high against the euro in early European dealing Tuesday, sending the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as low as Y120.00. The yen has also hit a series of five-month highs against the dollar recently, driving the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as low as Y113.60 earlier in the month. \par \par \uc2  Analysts have been quick to attribute both moves to institutional investors waking up to an economic recovery story in Japan. On Friday, Tokyo heightened expectations another notch by revising upward an already surprising growth rate for the first quarter. Gross domestic product grew at 2.0%, up from the 1.9% growth rate that dropped jaws when it was first announced earlier this summer. \par \par \uc2  As traders continue to report a flood of orders for yen, however, European investors and fund managers say they don't expect the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's quick rise to continue for long. \par \par \uc2  "We're not convinced that it's necessarily going to be sustained," says Norman McChesney, a fund manager responsible for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Aberdeen Asset Management in London. \par \par \uc2  Some of Aberdeen's funds have corrected long-standing underweight positions in Japan recently, buying up yen in order to invest in Japanese stocks and bonds. \par \par \uc2  Bank research suggests that other funds have done the same. Merrill Lynch wrote in a research note last week that, according to its latest Global Investor Survey, institutional funds have significantly increased yen holdings in the last quarter, bringing their portfolios closer to a neutral position than at any time in the last two years. \par \par \uc2  But Aberdeen's McChesney and investment managers like him in Europe say the recent rebalancing act has been slow and measured. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "We haven't entered into any yen positions yet," says Marco Bozzolan,\par a partner at Perreard Partners Investment, a Geneva-based\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -investment firm.\par         Recent Yen Rally May Already Be Over\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japanese investors, however, have clearly been favoring their domestic\par market at the expense of some of their euro holdings. But with so many\par dollars and euros already swapped for yen in the last two months, there\par may not be much room for further yen appreciation in the short-term,\par participants say.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "We've seen big selling already" in both dollar-yen and euro-yen\par markets, says Tilman Soeffker, head of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trading at Banque\par Nationale de Paris in Frankfurt, making predictions of further yen gains\par difficult to justify.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I'd question whether one can expect that much more flow away from\par Europe to Japan," Aberdeen's McChesney agrees.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Jeremy Hawkins, chief economist at BankAmerica in London, says that\par while many investors may be bringing their Japanese and Asian holding up\par to neutral weighting again, few are likely to jump in and start going\par overweight in yen-denominated assets anytime soon.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "People are trying to get a more balanced portfolio," he says.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Hawkins adds that while the yen could still climb higher against the\par dollar and euro - possible knocking the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as low as Y110 in\par the near-term - the yen's strength could start eating into profits at\par Japanese exporters and erode corporate balance sheets, reversing some of\par the very gains in Japanese asset market that have made the yen so\par attractive recently.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I think there's a real chance that that may stop the rally in the\par Nikkei," Hawkins says.\par        \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Still On Investors' Minds\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par And, despite the absence of the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in overt\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  recently, market watchers are still\par convinced Japanese monetary officials remain concerned that the yen\par could strengthen too quickly and strangle what now looks like a\par convincing economic turnaround.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Those fears abated somewhat Tuesday after comments by the head of the\par BOJ were interpreted as a sign the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  isn't close to\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in support of either the dollar or the euro.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Bank Governor Masaru Hayami said \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that targets a specific\par rate wasn't a good idea, and he told Japanese corporations that they\par should be prepared to live with a stronger yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Both comments were seized upon by European traders early Tuesday as a\par sign that the bank isn't poised to jump into \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets\par again.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But fear of getting caught on the wrong side of a BOJ \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  -\par no matter how remote - will likely keep many investors away from\par long-yen positions.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "We still believe there is an interest by Japanese officials to\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or at least to talk the yen down," says Perreard's Bozzolan.\par "We don't think we're at the moment when we should buy yen."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par At around 1400 GMT, the dollar was trading at Y114.33, and the euro\par was at Y120.14.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Chip Cummins; 44-171-842-9316; chip.cummins@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 17-08-99\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1403GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1196 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv8h064b5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
