\par\par \b \uc2  BOJ Watch: Hayami Urges Japan Cos To Endure Strong Yen \b0\par\par\uc2  By Kenneth McCallum \par \uc2 667 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 August 1999\par 12:54\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Ask not what the Bank of Japan can do to help the nation's manufacturers endure the strong yen. \par \par \uc2  That was the message from BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami, who urged companies Tuesday to take matters into their own hands by improving their competitiveness and shielding \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the impact of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuations. \par \par \uc2  "Authorities can't do something each time (there is a change in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) levels," the Governor said in his monthly press conference. \par \par \uc2  Therefore, Japanese companies "should strive to think of ways to make their management not affected by (changes in) \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though this is a thing that would require effort," he said. \par \par \uc2  Hayami's remarks came as the euro dropped to an all time low against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , at Y120.51 in late Tokyo trading. The dollar, already sluggish, fell immediately after the comments were reported. \par \par \uc2  The remarks were an indication that the BOJ doesn't share the same level of concern about a strong yen that is often expressed by officials in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But they don't necessarily provide any clues as to \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, which is set by the Finance Ministry. \par \par \uc2  Top MOF officials have repeatedly said that a premature strengthening of the yen is undesirable, though Japanese authorities haven't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  recently to prevent the rise in the yen. \par \par \uc2  In the past, BOJ officials have also said that a yen strengthening is undesirable, because it would worsen deflation by pulling down import prices. \par \par \uc2  But BOJ officials have said little about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates recently, though the BOJ's monthly report released earlier Tuesday said that the yen has strengthened on the back of optimism about the Japanese economy and worries about U.S. inflation. \par \par \uc2  At his press conference, Hayami said that in general, Japan will continue to carefully monitor the yen. \par \par \uc2  But he evaded a reporter's question about whether Japan and the U.S. differ in their views on acceptable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate levels, saying only that the two governments in principle share the view that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates should reflect economic fundamentals and move in a stable manner. \par \par \uc2  Hayami, a well-known proponent of a strong yen before he was appointed Governor early last year, said that Japan needs to increase the amount of exports it settles in yen in order for Japanese companies to be hurt less by a rise in the yen. \par \par \uc2  He noted that while Japan only settles about 36% of its total exports in yen, Germany settles around 75% of its exports in Deutsche marks. \par \par \uc2  Although Japanese companies may, in the long run, become better at resisting the damage of the strong yen, they are likely to be hit by the current appreciation of the home \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Many \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders believe that the yen will strengthen further on the back of bullishness about the Japanese economy and stock market, and worries about U.S. inflation and the effect of a possible U.S. rate hike on U.S. asset markets. \par \par \uc2  And the BOJ showed Tuesday that it's much more cautious than the market regarding the state of the economy. \par \par \uc2  In its August economic report, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left its overall assessment of the economy unchanged, saying "Japan's economy, at present, has stopped deteriorating, and corporate sentiment has seen a slight improvement recently." \par \par \uc2  But the BOJ repeated "it is still difficult to expect an immediate self-sustained recovery in private demand," because corporate restructuring is expected to reduce business fixed investment and depress employment and income conditions. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Hayami also laid to rest market speculation that the BOJ might be\par closer to abandoning its ultra-easy monetary policy, reiterating that\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will maintain its current policy until worries about\par deflation subside, something which he said hasn't happened yet.\par -By Kenneth McCallum; 813-5255-2938; kmccallum@ap.org\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 17-08-99\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1054GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1042 4131 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv8h0640z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
