\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-BOJ puts policy on hold, warns on budget. \b0\par\par\uc2  By William Mallard \par \uc2 725 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 August 1999\par 11:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Aug 17 (Reuters) - Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami said on Tuesday the BOJ would keep interest rates at rock bottom, while cautioning the government not to expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cover for another fiscal spending spree. \par \par \uc2  "We will maintain our policy until deflationary fears abate," Hayami said, adding that downward price pressures persist as supply still greatly outstrips economic demand. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ's six-month-old policy of driving overnight interest rates essentially to zero has helped the economy and financial markets, he told a regular news conference, adding that he hoped the benefits would continue to spread. \par \par \uc2  But he emphasised his concern that politicians might blow a hole in the budget deficit with another spending package and pressure the BOJ to hold down the higher interest rates by buying the resulting government debt. \par \par \uc2  Hayami declined comment on an expected supplementary budget for the fiscal year through next March but said he was very interested in its size and how it would be financed. \par \par \uc2  "It is clear that Japan's fiscal burden has become very high compared with other countries," he said, noting that the total public sector debt exceeds annual gross domestic product. \par \par \uc2  He reiterated the BOJ's flat rejection of proposals from some ruling party politicians for the BOJ to cap long-term interest rates by buying debt directly from the government or expanding its government bond purchases in the secondary market. \par \par \uc2  Given weak private-sector demand for funds and a continued excess of savings over investment in Japan, he added, it was difficult to imagine that concerns of government bond oversupply alone could push up long-term interest rates sharply. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the day, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  maintained in a monthly report its assessment that: "The economy has stopped deteriorating and corporate sentiment has improved somewhat, but no clear movements toward a self-sustaining recovery in private demand are visible." \par \par \uc2  The BOJ said consumer sentiment has improved due to better conditions in the financial system, but this has not led to a recovery in consumption as joblessness and personal income conditions continue to worsen. \par \par \uc2  Hayami also said it was noteworthy that industrial production was hitting a floor and turning upward. \par \par \uc2  Separately, the BOJ said the key gauge of money supply rose 4.0 percent in July from a year earlier. This was at the low end of eight forecasts in a Reuters survey, which on average predicted M2+CDs would expand by 4.3 percent. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ said money supply growth was largely on a flat trend, while economists said the data showed bank lending remains stunted by weak fund demand. \par \par \uc2  On another worrying factor, Hayami said there were limits to what the authorities could do to combat the persistent strength in the yen, which has risen not only against the dollar but also set a lifetime high against the euro earlier on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  A high yen can hurt Japan's exports, he said, but he urged companies to make greater efforts to shield themselves from \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  swings, such as by denominating more of their international settlements in yen. \par \par \uc2  "Corporations need to make more efforts to consider management methods so that they will not be affected by \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuations," he said. \par \par \uc2  He also downplayed talk that Japanese and U.S. authorities differ on appropriate \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels, saying: "Both countries have agreed that it is favourable for exchange rates to reflect economic fundamentals and move in a stable manner." \par \par \uc2  Kazuhiko Ogata, senior economist at ABN Amro Securities in Tokyo, said that sooner or later Japanese, U.S. and European authorities are likely to agree to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to curb the yen. \par \par \uc2  The strong yen, in addition to increasing deflationary pressures, hurts the economy by making the nation's exports more expensive overseas and eroding the yen value of firms' overseas earnings. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly after June 10 data showed a stunning jump in January-March gross domestic product, at one point forcing the dollar above 122 yen. But the authorities have not been seen in the market for nearly four weeks as the dollar has slid back. It was quoted around 114.70 yen late in Tuesday. ((Tokyo newsroom +81-3 3432-8022 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv8h02k09\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
