\par\par \b \uc2  Japan Euro sales seen offset by Europe recovery. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Masayuki Kitano \par \uc2 774 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 August 1999\par 11:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Aug 17 (Reuters) - Potential hedge selling of the euro by Japanese institutions is seen being offset by the effects of emerging economic recovery in Europe, and this should prevent the euro falling sharply below 120 yen, investors and dealers in Tokyo say. Signs of a peaking out of European investors' demand for Japanese stocks, at least until fresh signs of an upturn in the Japanese economy emerge, are also likely to prevent the yen from rising sharply against the euro, they said. \par \par \uc2  The euro fell to a record low of 120.77 yen in early Asian trading on Tuesday, sparking fresh speculation of hedge-selling needs among Japanese investors and exporters. \par \par \uc2  Toru Umemoto, a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist for Morgan Stanley Bank AG in Tokyo, said hedge-selling needs among Japanese investors were likely to remain strong. \par \par \uc2  "I think the weighted average of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  cost of euro-denominated (securities) investments conducted by Japanese investors last year was around 145 to 147 yen," Umemoto said. \par \par \uc2  An international investment official at Nippon Life Insurance Co agreed that more hedge selling of the euro by Japanese institutional investors would probably emerge. \par \par \uc2  "I think there will be more hedge selling of the euro by Japanese investors, using instruments such as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  forwards and options," the Nippon Life official said. \par \par \uc2  But he said the euro's falls against the yen were likely to be limited unless more signs of a recovery in Japan emerge and encourage overseas players to buy more yen. \par \par \uc2  Dealers and analysts said hopes for a recovery in euro zone economies were likely to mitigate against the impact of Japanese institutions' hedge-selling of the euro. \par \par \uc2  Those hopes were boosted by a stronger-than-expected June business climate survey released last month by Germany's Ifo economic research institute indicating Europe's largest economy was recovering after more than six months of stagnation. \par \par \uc2  "Both Japan and Europe seem headed towards economic recovery, and since Europe's economic prospects do not look too bad, I don't think the yen will rise sharply against the euro," said Morgan Stanley's Umemoto. \par \par \uc2  Market players said the potential for yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also likely to prevent the euro from falling sharply against the yen. \par \par \uc2  Senior \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official Haruhiko Kuroda said there was no change in Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy despite the euro's fall against the yen to a record low earlier. \par \par \uc2  MOF officials have repeatedly said a premature appreciation of the yen is undesirable as it could nip Japan's economic recovery in the bud. \par \par \uc2  Kigae Kwon, an executive director for UBS in Tokyo, said falls in the euro against the yen from current levels were also likely to be limited as European investors' demand for Japanese stocks seems to have peaked, at least for the time being. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think Japanese stock investment by European investors from now will take place on the same magnitude as we saw at the start of the year," Kwon said. \par \par \uc2  Due to their investment in Japanese equities since the start of the year, European institutional investors are, for the most part, no longer underweighted in Japanese stocks, Kwon said. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think they have enough confidence in Japan's macroeconomic conditions to go overweight in Japanese stocks," Kwon said. \par \par \uc2  Overseas investors purchased a net 5.8637 trillion yen in Japanese stocks from January to July, MOF statistics showed. \par \par \uc2  Another positive sign for the euro was that Japanese investors have been net buyers of foreign bonds issued by entities in major euro zone countries in recent months, despite speculation to the contrary. \par \par \uc2  "Japanese investors...started buying euro-denominated assets a bit too early," with their active buying of euro zone securities last year, said Akihiko Kagawa, chief manager at the Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and treasury division. \par \par \uc2  But their demand for such assets for diversification away from U.S. assets is likely to pick up again in the future, as the euro's standing gradually ascends to rival the dollar's status as an anchor \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Kagawa said. \par \par \uc2  MOF's latest data shows Japanese investors bought a net 665.3 billion yen worth of German, French and Dutch bonds in June, while selling a net 128.9 billion yen worth of U.S. bonds. \par \par \uc2  The euro was quoted at 120.63/71 yen and the dollar stood at 114.69/74 yen as of 0937 GMT. \par \par \uc2  ((Masayuki Kitano, Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 5473 3748 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv8h02g5g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
