\par\par \b \uc2  New Optimism On Japan Recovery Spurs Yen Buying Vs Euro \b0\par\par\uc2  By Marianne Sullivan \par \uc2 742 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 August 1999\par 21:33\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Yen became the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy Monday after Japan's strong growth numbers and the resultant rally in the country's stocks seemed the last bit of convincing investors needed that Japanese recovery is on track. \par \par \uc2  But with U.S. markets and the dollar also staging a comeback, the euro was the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell, falling to an all-time low of Y121.10 during the New York session. \par \par \uc2  "We are back to the Japanese recovery story, and so people want to be long in Japanese equities," said Tomas Jelf, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Warburg Dillon Read in Stamford. CT. "But in the U.S., sentiment has also improved, so investors are unwilling to buy yen against the dollar. The one unknown is European recovery and that is not as convincing yet," \par \par \uc2  At 1925 GMT, the dollar is trading at Y114.65, down from Y115.72 late Friday. The euro is trading at $1.0579 up from $1.0568 late Friday and at Y121.29, down from Y122.29 Friday. \par \par \uc2  Friday's upward revision in Japan's first-quarter gross domestic product growth to 2.0% from 1.9% surprised market players expecting the already strong growth outlook to be revised downward. Monday's revision in the country's June industrial output gain to 3.2% from a preliminary 3.0% added to the enthusiasm, helping to push Japan's 225 stock index up 2.24% and the dollar and euro about one yen lower in Asian trade. \par \par \uc2  Lisa Finstrom, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Salomon Smith Barney in New York, said a real shift in institutional attitude in underway regarding Japan. \par \par \uc2  "It appears that there is less skepticism, and that real changes are underway," Finstrom said. \par \par \uc2  Even before investors were completely convinced in Japan's recovery, there was a significant move into the country's Nikkei stock index, which has gained 26% this year. Bruce Giammettei, senior trader at Mellon Bank in Pittsburgh, said his clients have been increasingly moving to square underweight portfolio positions in Japan. \par \par \uc2  The eurozone has also been seeing some earlier-than-expected signs of growth and while these signs have been enough to lift the euro off its all-time low, they aren't sufficient to convince investors to increase their positions in European equities. \par \par \uc2  In 1999, Germany's Xetra DAX index is up only 4.25% while France's CAC 40 is 12.47% higher. The Dow Jones Industrial Average is up about 20% for the year. \par \par \uc2  The euro's rise to $1.0824 on August 5 from a low of $1.0108 on July 13 was mostly the result of investors adjusting their euro hedges on equity positions, according to traders. \par \par \uc2  But analysts seem to agree there won't be many definitive moves in such light summer trading. Investors are also awaiting key economic data in the U.S. and Europe before making any rushed investment decisions. \par \par \uc2  U.S. asset markets and thus the dollar have become hostage of changing expectations of increases in interest rates. U.S. consumer prices due Tuesday could thus have a significant impact on forecasts, with markets looking for confirmation of mild price pressure. Likewise, Germany's Ifo index due Thursday should lend further insight into eurozone recovery. The same day, U.S. trade data will be closely watched now that the U.S. current-account deficit is cited as a weight on the dollar. \par \par \uc2  And lest market players forget, there is always the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . On behalf of Japanese monetary authorities, the BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  seven times between June 10 and July 21 to halt a premature appreciation of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But the fear the market once had of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be waning. \par \par \uc2  "The funny thing," said WDR's Jelf, "is that the big \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  have come in dollar/yen. The BOJ's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in euro/yen was not really enough for the market to remember it and was not that successful." \par \par \uc2  The BOJ's ability to affect the yen's trading range has also been undermined by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's insistence that the funds used for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  be sterilized, meaning that liquidity is not actually added to the system. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "This substantially takes away the price threat to the market," said\par Finstrom.\par -By Marianne Sullivan; 201-938-4385\par email:marianne.sullivan@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 16-08-99\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1933GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv8g062jg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
