\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Dollar firm, basks in benign data. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,007 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 1999\par 17:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 13 (Reuters) - The dollar ended the week in Europe on a bullish note as markets welcomed U.S. statistics which showed weak inflationary pressures and pointed to a minor monetary tightening later this month, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar turned around after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell overnight against the yen on an upward revision to 2.0 percent in Japanese first-quarter gross domestic product growth. \par \par \uc2  "PPI was a good benign number, the dollar seems to have risen on that," said John Calverley, chief economist and strategist at American Express. "We're in a picture where the economy is strong but inflation still isn't a problem". \par \par \uc2  As the dollar climbed against the euro and other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the back of weaker-than-expected wholesale prices data, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  spiked up a yen against the Japanese unit, amid jitters over Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders said the sharp rise in dollar/yen was due to short-covering rather than \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese authorities, who want to weaken the yen to ensure a fragile export-led recovery is not blown off course by yen strength. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 115.78/83 yen at 1451 GMT, towards the upper end of the day's near-two-yen range, the largest one-day trading range so far this month. \par \par \uc2  Euro/dollar fell to 2-1/2 week lows of $1.0566/73 at 1459 GMT compared with $1.0631/33 in late European trading on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The 0.2 percent rise in the July U.S. producer prices index reassured investors the economy was not overheating and the Federal Reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would only raise interest rates by a modest quarter percentage point. \par \par \uc2  "It probably doesn't change the idea that Fed funds will go up at the end of August, and possibly one more time, to get back to where we were last July," Calverly said. He expects the Fed to tighten by an initial 25 basis points, then a further 25. \par \par \uc2  He predicted euro/dollar, dollar/yen and sterling would be close to current levels in two to three months. \par \par \uc2  "We're going to stay poised between the Fed raising but by not very much and inflation threatening but not coming through in the next couple of months," he said. \par \par \uc2  In the euro zone, investors received mixed signals on growth and inflation. \par \par \uc2  Germany reported retail sales grew a real 3.8 percent year-on-year, providing evidence of growth in the region's largest economy. \par \par \uc2  But Spain and Portugal posted July inflation data which were stuck at the same level as the previous month, respectively 2.2 and 2.1 percent. \par \par \uc2  Technical analysts are watching euro/dollar to see if it hits $1.0550, a closely watched level which marks a 38.2 percent retracement on the euro's rally from life lows in July. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the dollar's spike up against the yen in the wake of the PPI figures was due to a large U.S. investment bank buying at around 115.20 yen, accelerating the dollar's sudden climb. \par \par \uc2  They added there was an absence of sell orders usually left by Japanese exporters, providing little resistance to the dollar's spike up. \par \par \uc2  "We've been seeing a lot of sell orders left up around 117 yen, but there seems to have been nothing in between," said a dealer at a Japanese bank in London. \par \par \uc2  He said the Japanese manufacturing companies apparently had had removed their sell orders at nearby levels ahead of their summer vacation. Japan's traditional "o-bon" holiday period typically peaks around August 15. ((Pierre Tran, London Capital Markets +44 171 542 2737, pierre.tran@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  The 0.2 percent rise in the July U.S. producer prices index reassured investors the economy was not overheating and the Federal Reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would only raise interest rates by a modest quarter percentage point. \par \par \uc2  "It probably doesn't change the idea that Fed funds will go up at the end of August, and possibly one more time, to get back to where we were last July," Calverly said. He expects the Fed to tighten by an initial 25 basis points, then a further 25. \par \par \uc2  He predicted euro/dollar, dollar/yen and sterling would be close to current levels in two to three months. \par \par \uc2  "We're going to stay poised between the Fed raising but by not very much and inflation threatening but not coming through in the next couple of months," he said. \par \par \uc2  In the euro zone, investors received mixed signals on growth and inflation. \par \par \uc2  Germany reported retail sales grew a real 3.8 percent year-on-year, providing evidence of growth in the region's largest economy. \par \par \uc2  But Spain and Portugal posted July inflation data which were stuck at the same level as the previous month, respectively 2.2 and 2.1 percent. \par \par \uc2  Technical analysts are watching euro/dollar to see if it hits $1.0550, a closely watched level which marks a 38.2 percent retracement on the euro's rally from life lows in July. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the dollar's spike up against the yen in the wake of the PPI figures was due to a large U.S. investment bank buying at around 115.20 yen, accelerating the dollar's sudden climb. \par \par \uc2  They added there was an absence of sell orders usually left by Japanese e