\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Fragile dollar awaits U.S. data. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,081 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 1999\par 13:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 13 (Reuters) - The dollar rose from earlier lows set against the euro and the yen on Friday, but struggled to make headway as dealers were reluctant to buy dollars ahead of U.S. data which may give clues to the direction of U.S. rates. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said July U.S. production prices, due out at 1230 GMT, would be key to assessing whether inflationary pressures are strong enough to prompt the Federal Reserve to hike interest rates at its August 24 policy meeting. \par \par \uc2  Stronger-than-expected data could lead to a sell-off in U.S. assets which would have a negative impact on the dollar, they said. \par \par \uc2  "Recent U.S. surveys all point to inflationary pressures in the U.S.," said Claudio Piron, treasury economist at Standard Chartered in London. "The market is hesistant to hold dollars ahead of the PPI data." \par \par \uc2  By 1030 GMT, the euro was at $1.0670/74 from $1.0631/33 late in Europe on Thursday but remained below the session peak of $1.0697 hit after the release of strong German retail sales. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at 114.83/92 yen from 115.76/86 late in Europe on Thursday and around one percent below the two-week peak of 116.04 set on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar slide against the yen was triggered overnight by an unexpected upward revision of Japanese January-March gross domestic product figures. Dealers who had bet on a downward revision to the GDP data scrambled to buy yen after GDP growth was revised to 2.0 percent from a previous 1.9 percent estimate. \par \par \uc2  Stronger-than-expected German retail sales had lifted euro/dollar early in the European session, but their impact was short-lived as analysts said the data were erratic. \par \par \uc2  Still, they added that the improved outlook for the consumer sector helped to offset weaker-than-expected industrial German production data released earlier this week. \par \par \uc2  "The corner has been turned for the European economy and for the euro," said Andrew Snowball, senior economist at Julius Baer Investment in London. "The euro will be supported, but it's going to be a stroll rather than a spring up." \par \par \uc2  Euro zone June industry PPI rose by 0.1 percent in June from May but fell by 1.0 percent year-on-year. The data had limited impact on euro/dollar, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Snowball at Julius Baer Investments said that the market would turn its attention on the next IFO business confidence survey in Germany. \par \par \uc2  A very optimistic report released in July provided solid foundations for a seven-cent rally in the euro in less than a month. Analysts said they now want to see if the new report can match the July one. \par \par \uc2  But for Friday, dealers and analysts expected euro/dollar and dollar/yen to trade quitely in ranges until the release of the U.S data. \par \par \uc2  One dealer at a U.S. investment bank in London said the ongoing tensions between Taiwan and China were one factor limiting interest in the yen. He also cited resurfacing fears that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yen. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile the dollar also lost ground against the Swiss franc and sterling. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  retreated towards the 1.50-franc area while sterling was back above $1.61. \par \par \uc2  The Swedish crown rose against the dollar and was steady against the euro after the Swedish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  warned that stronger-than-expected growth in Sweden might result in inflationary pressures. \par \par \uc2  Its comments fuelled speculation that an interest rate rise was in the pipeline. \par \par \uc2  By 1030 GMT, the euro was at $1.0670/74 from $1.0631/33 late in Europe on Thursday but remained below the session peak of $1.0697 hit after the release of strong German retail sales. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at 114.83/92 yen from 115.76/86 late in Europe on Thursday and around one percent below the two-week peak of 116.04 set on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar slide against the yen was triggered overnight by an unexpected upward revision of Japanese January-March gross domestic product figures. Dealers who had bet on a downward revision to the GDP data scrambled to buy yen after GDP growth was revised to 2.0 percent from a previous 1.9 percent estimate. \par \par \uc2  Stronger-than-expected German retail sales had lifted euro/dollar early in the European session, but their impact was short-lived as analysts said the data were erratic. \par \par \uc2  Still, they added that the improved outlook for the consumer sector helped to offset weaker-than-expected industrial German production data released earlier this week. "The corner has been turned for the European economy and for the euro," said Andrew Snowball, senior economist at Julius Baer Investment in London. "The euro will be supported, but it's going to be a stroll rather than a spring up." \par \par \uc2  Euro zone June industry PPI rose by 0.1 percent in June from May but fell by 1.0 percent year-on-year. The data had limited impact on euro/dollar, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Snowball at Julius Baer Investments said that the market would turn its attention on the next IFO business confidence survey in Germany. \par \par \uc2  A very optimistic report released in July provided solid foundations for a seven-cent rally in the euro in less than a month. Analysts said they now want to see if the new report can match the July one. \par \par \uc2  But for Friday, dealers and analysts expected euro/dollar and dollar/yen to trade quitely in ranges until the release of the U.S data. \par \par \uc2  One dealer at a U.S. investment bank in London said the ongoing tensions between Taiwan and China were one factor limiting interest in the yen. He also cited resurfacing fears that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yen. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile the dollar also lost ground against the Swiss franc and sterling. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  retreated towards the 1.50-franc area while sterling was back above $1.61. \par \par \uc2  The Swedish crown rose against the dollar and was steady against the euro after the Swedish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  warned that stronger-than-expected growth in Sweden might result in inflationary pressures. Its comments fuelled speculation that an interest rate rise was in the pipeline. \par \par \uc2  ((Lisa Jucca, London Capital Markets 0171 542 6745, elisabetta.jucca@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv8d02o2p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
