\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE-Strong German, Japan data dent dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2 952 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 1999\par 10:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 13 (Reuters) - The dollar was on the retreat on Friday as economic data from Germany and Japan lifted the euro and the yen. \par \par \uc2  Stronger than expected German retail sales offset the negative impact of soft German industrial output figures released earlier this week. But analysts said the euro's ability to extend its gains would depend on further confirmation of a recovery in the euro zone economy. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the upward revision of Japanese first quarter gross domestic product data boosted the yen and forced the dollar to abandon a two-week peak above 116 yen set on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  German June retail sales rose a nominal 2.7 percent year-on-year in June against a 3.1 percent drop the month before. In real terms, June sales were up 3.8 percent compared with a 2.5 percent fall in May. \par \par \uc2  "The German figures were a little bit on the strong side and helped the euro to gain some ground against the dollar," said Michael Rottmann, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at HypoVereinsbank in Munich. \par \par \uc2  "But we have to wait until September to see a substantial recovery in Germany. We are one or two months too early." \par \par \uc2  In Japan, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  revised GDP growth to 2.0 percent from a previous 1.9 percent. The revision surprised the market which had been speculating there would be a downward adjustment. \par \par \uc2  By 0730 GMT, the euro was at $1.0680/85 compared with $1.0631/33 late in Europe on Thursday. The euro rose as far as $1.0697 soon after the release of the German data, but was capped by a chart barrier at $1.0710, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the data confirmed that the outlook for the consumer sector in the euro zone's biggest economy was positive. But they said more economic evidence was needed to allow the euro to rise further as retail sales were a volatile indicator. \par \par \uc2  "For the time being the feeling that the European recovery is underway is in place," said Ian Gunner, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN AMRO in London. \par \par \uc2  "But we need to go beyond there. We need a feeling of strong recovery (in Europe) or a fall in U.S. assets." \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile the dollar was at 114.75/80 yen from 115.76/86 yen late in Europe on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has shed one percent from a two-week peak of 116.04 yen hit on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said resurfacing fears that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the strengthening yen had halted the dollar's slide at chart support around 114.50 yen. \par \par \uc2  Wariness of the BOJ was fuelled after Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said on Friday he was always watching the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market carefully. \par \par \uc2  "It's going to be difficult for the BOJ to turn dollar/yen around," said Gunner at ABN Amro. "Japan, on its own, cannot do much. A successful campaign of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  really requires the Fed's help." \par \par \uc2  The dollar also lost ground against the Swiss franc and sterling. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  retreated towards the 1.50 franc area while sterling was back above $1.61. \par \par \uc2  The next key focus for the market will be the release of U.S. July PPI data at 1230 GMT, which could reinforce the view that the Fed is set to raise rates in August if they are stronger than expected, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Euro zone June PPI is out at 1000 GMT. \par \par \uc2  By 0730 GMT, the euro was at $1.0680/85 compared with $1.0631/33 late in Europe on Thursday. The euro rose as far as $1.0697 soon after the release of the German data, but was capped by a chart barrier at $1.0710, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the data confirmed that the outlook for the consumer sector in the euro zone's biggest economy was positive. But they said more economic evidence was needed to allow the euro to rise further as retail sales were a volatile indicator. \par \par \uc2  "For the time being the feeling that the European recovery is underway is in place," said Ian Gunner, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN AMRO in London. \par \par \uc2  "But we need to go beyond there. We need a feeling of strong recovery (in Europe) or a fall in U.S. assets." \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile the dollar was at 114.75/80 yen from 115.76/86 yen late in Europe on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has shed one percent from a two-week peak of 116.04 yen hit on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said resurfacing fears that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the strengthening yen had halted the dollar's slide at chart support around 114.50 yen. \par \par \uc2  Wariness of the BOJ was fuelled after Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said on Friday he was always watching the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market carefully. \par \par \uc2  "It's going to be difficult for the BOJ to turn dollar/yen around," said Gunner at ABN Amro. "Japan, on its own, cannot do much. A successful campaign of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  really requires the Fed's help." \par \par \uc2  The dollar also lost ground against the Swiss franc and sterling. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  retreated towards the 1.50 franc area while sterling was back above $1.61. \par \par \uc2  The next key focus for the market will be the release of U.S. July PPI data at 1230 GMT, which could reinforce the view that the Fed is set to raise rates in August if they are stronger than expected, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Euro zone June PPI is out at 1000 GMT. \par \par \uc2  ((Lisa Jucca, London Capital Markets 0171 542 6745, elisabetta.jucca@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv8d02n4k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
