\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar down vs yen on Japan GDP surprise, off lows. \b0\par\par\uc2 693 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 1999\par 09:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Aug 13 (Reuters) - The dollar was weaker versus yen in late Tokyo trade on Friday but off its earlier lows, as the market digested an unexpected upward revision in Japan's gross domestic product (GDP) data for the January-March quarter. \par \par \uc2  The dollar slid almost one yen after Japan's Economic Planning Agency (EPA) revised the January-March GDP figure to a quarter-on-quarter rise of 2.0 percent from a preliminary rise of 1.9 prcent. But the greenback later recaptured some of its losses, buoyed by solid bids around 114.50 yen, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 114.84/89 yen at 0622 GMT, down from 115.50/60 yen in New York late on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell as low as 114.48 yen after the release of the GDP data, which took many operators off guard as they had been expecting a weaker figure after rumours spread overnight that the GDP might be revised sharply downward. \par \par \uc2  But some wariness about the possibility of dollar-buying yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bids probably placed by Japanese importers and investors gave the dollar support, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "We've recovered from the GDP shock and are standing at another starting point. But the next step doesn't seem so clear now," said a senior dealer at a U.S. bank. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the GDP's impact on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market was also tempered by the relatively cool reaction of Japanese stocks and bonds. \par \par \uc2  The benchmark Nikkei average of 225 leading shares posted a minor gain to close at 17,435.17, up 12.20 points or 0.07 percent. \par \par \uc2  The yield of the August 10-year government bond stood at 1.855 percent, up 0.03 from late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  A city bank dealer said: "The size of the GDP revision is too small to have a lasting impact on markets. I think reactions in the stock and bond markets were more logical." \par \par \uc2  EPA Minister Taichi Sakaiya said after the GDP announcement that he remained sure Japan's economy in fiscal 1999/00 would hit the government's 0.5 percent growth target. \par \par \uc2  Sakaiya also said the revised GDP figures had not changed his views on the economy, or on the need for an extra budget. \par \par \uc2  Another Japanese bank dealer said: "Despite all the fuss over the data (in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market), there's basically no change in prospects for the Japanese economy before and after the data." \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market would be watching the outcome of the BOJ's policy-setting meeting on Friday, although most players expected the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep its policy unchanged. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ said the results of the meeting would be announced at 0715 GMT. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think the BOJ will take additional easing steps, because if it does, it could trigger yen buying on hopes of improvement in the Japanese economy," the third dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The market will be also watching U.S. producer price index (PPI) data for July, due out at 1230 GMT on Friday, and its effect on U.S. asset markets, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Economists polled by Reuters forecast, on average, that the PPI rose 0.3 percent overall and 0.1 percent excluding food and energy. \par \par \uc2  Trading was unaffected by news that the Liberal Party, the junior partner in Japan's ruling coalition, will stay allied with Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi's Liberal Democratic Party. If the Liberal Party had decided to leave the coalition, it could have triggered yen selling, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The market showed a muted reaction to remarks after the GDP data by senior Finance Ministry official Zembei Mizoguchi, who reiterated that a premature rise in the yen before Japan's economy has recovered is undesirable and that the authorities will take appropriate steps to deal with it if needed. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $1.0673/80 at 0710 GMT, up slightly from $1.0661/68 in New York late on Thursday. ((Risa Maeda, Tokyo Treasury Desk 81-3-3432-8570 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv8d02mnz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
