\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr dips on Japan GDP, wary over possible BOJ move. \b0\par\par\uc2 725 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 1999\par 06:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Aug 13 (Reuters) - The dollar was down against the yen at midday in Tokyo on Friday, after the market was surprised by an upward revision in Japan's gross domestic product (GDP) data for the January-March quarter. \par \par \uc2  But the greenback pared earlier losses on concerns about possible dollar-buying, yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ), dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 114.80/90 yen at 0314 GMT, down from 115.50/60 yen in New York late on Thursday. It dropped as low as 114.48 yen after the GDP data's release, down nearly one yen from an intraday high of 115.41 yen earlier on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Japan's Economic Planning Agency said on Friday that GDP rose a revised 2.0 percent in real terms in the January-March quarter from the previous quarter, up from 1.9 percent growth in the preliminary estimate released in June. \par \par \uc2  "The revision was very small, but a lot of people were geared up for a weak figure after rumours spread overnight that GDP might be revised sharply downward," said a trust bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  Those rumours briefly lifted the dollar to 116.05 yen in Europe on Thursday. But the upside was blocked by sell orders above 116 yen left by Japanese exporters, many of which are now away for Japan's "bon" summer holiday season. \par \par \uc2  After diving in a knee-jerk reaction to the GDP data, the dollar found some support on dealer buybacks and sporadic bargain hunting by Japanese investors. \par \par \uc2  "People remember well that the BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars aggressively after the yen was boosted by the preliminary GDP data. So they were reluctant to chase the dollar too far down," said a dealer at a city bank. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the GDP's impact on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market was also tempered by its relatively cool impact on Japanese stocks and bonds. \par \par \uc2  At midday, the benchmark Nikkei average of 225 leading shares was down 82.93 points or 0.48 percent at 17,340.04. \par \par \uc2  The yield of the August 10-year government bond stood at 1.845 percent, up 0.02 from late on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  A city bank dealer said: "The size of the GDP revision is too small to have a lasting impact on markets. I think reactions in stock and bond markets were more logical." \par \par \uc2  Trading was unaffected by news that the Liberal Party, the junior partner in Japan's ruling coalition, will stay in the alliance with Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi's Liberal Democratic Party. \par \par \uc2  "If the Liberals decided to leave the coalition, it might have weighed on the yen, as political instability would hamper smooth implementation of future economic policy steps," the city bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "News that the coalition will stay intact just wasn't exciting enough to move the market," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market would be watching U.S. producer price index (PPI) data for July, due later on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Economists polled by Reuters forecast, on average, that the PPI rose 0.3 percent overall and 0.1 percent excluding food and energy. \par \par \uc2  "Whatever the outcome, the PPI is not expected to alter the market's expectations that the Federal Reserve will raise interest rates on August 24," said a trust bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  "So the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market's reaction would probably largely depend on how U.S. Treasuries move after the data," he added. \par \par \uc2  Overnight in New York, the dollar closed lower against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as prices of U.S. Treasuries fell on a disappointing last leg of the quarterly refunding auctions. \par \par \uc2  The market will also be watching the outcome of the BOJ's policy-setting meeting on Friday, although most players expected the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep its policy unchanged. \par \par \uc2  The market showed a muted reaction to earlier remarks by senior Finance Ministry official Zembei Mizoguchi, who reiterated that a premature rise in the yen before Japan's economy has recovered is undesirable and that the authorities will take appropriate steps to deal with it if needed. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $1.0681/84 at 0359 GMT, up slightly from $1.0661/68 in New York late on Thursday. ((Kanta Watanabe, Tokyo Treasury Desk 81-3-3432-9780 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv8d02mcl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
