\par\par \b \uc2  U.S. analysts say time not right for BoJ moves. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Elizabeth Lazarowitz \par \uc2 580 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 1999\par 20:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Aug 13 (Reuters) - Friday the 13th may have spooked some dealers into thinking the Bank of Japan engineered the yen's early morning dive, but analysts said the time was not right for another round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is certainly on the radar screen. But at this juncture, the urgency's not there," said Tomas Jelf, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Warburg Dillon Read, adding the Bank of Japan may not move before the strong yen takes another bite out of exports. \par \par \uc2  Fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  grew overnight when Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa again said he was watching the markets carefully, a message often interpreted by the markets as awarning that another \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  strike is imminent. \par \par \uc2  So when the dollar rushed up more than a full yen in a half-hour to a 116.20 yen session high, many dealers thought the Bank of Japan was deftly stepping into the markets as the dollar was already rising along with rallying asset markets. \par \par \uc2  But as the dollar then pared it losses almost as quickly as it had packed them on, dealers agreed there was no mysterious force today. It was only a large corporate order. \par \par \uc2  "The BoJ hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  because dollar/yen has been relatively stable in a three-yen range in past weeks," said Marc Chandler, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Mellon Bank of Pittsburgh. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the summer, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  often and fiercely, arguing that a premature rise in the yen would undermine the nation's fragile economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  But the Bank of Japan has been absent in the past few weeks as the U.S. economic outlook shifted, and more and more analysts became convinced the Federal Reserve will need to tighten credit at its August meeting to keep inflationary pressures under control. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has lost ground for some weeks, for reasons more related to U.S. problems as investors feared the flows of capital into the United States will slow if rates rise. \par \par \uc2  While higher interest rates often help attract outside investment, recently, fears of Fed tightening have unnerved asset markets, and may make U.S. stocks less alluring. \par \par \uc2  This means any attempts by Japan might make to bolster the yen could be futile as they would be moving against the market's direction. \par \par \uc2  At the moment, the Japanese officials "are actors in somebody else's play and they recognise this," said Paul Podolsky, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at BankBoston. "Everybody is really wondering what the Fed is going to do this August and in October. That is the focus right now." \par \par \uc2  Analysts agreed the Bank of Japan was unlikely to act without an even sharper rise in the yen than the one seen overnight on Friday coupled with a radical drop in Japan's Nikkei-225 stock index. \par \par \uc2  Overnight the dollar bounced to a 114.50 yen low after Japan's already strong 1.9 percent growth rate for the January through March period was revised to 2 percent. \par \par \uc2  At the same time analysts said the Bank of Japan would probably not act without the help of another \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , something which appears unlikely right now. \par \par \uc2  "I think it is unlikely that the U.S. would help now especially after President Bill Clinton said he was not alarmed by the dollar's recent decline," Citibank analyst Bob Sinche said. \par \par \uc2  ((-N.A. Treasury Desk, 212-859-1607 )). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv8d02gth\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
