\par\par \b \uc2  BOJ monetary policy seen unchanged at Fri meeting. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chikafumi Hodo \par \uc2 642 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 1999\par 09:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Aug 12 (Reuters) - The Bank of Japan is expected to stick with its monetary policy when its board meets on Friday, but analysts say market speculation persists about one more card the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could play to restrain the yen. \par \par \uc2  After \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets several times in June and July - which it does at the behest of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  - the BOJ constantly drained excess funds in order to keep a yen surplus in the money market stable at around one trillion yen. \par \par \uc2  Some market players suspect that so-called sterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , when the BOJ drains funds from the market after \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , may be stopped, as this could be more effective in weakening the yen and an indirect way to ease credit further. \par \par \uc2  "I think there's a consensus growing among \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers that the BOJ may be helpless in stemming the yen's strength," said Yoshito Sakakibara, vice president and senior economist at Goldman Sachs (\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 ). \par \par \uc2  "With \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the BOJ acting alone losing its effectiveness, the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market still speculates that unsterilising \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  is possible as a final option to halt the yen's rise, despite repeated denials from officials," said Sakakibara. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ Policy Board has a regular meeting on Friday, but analysts expect no change in its monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  "Present economic and \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  conditions suggest there is only a very slim chance the BOJ will take the unsterilising policy to ease further," Sakakibara said. \par \par \uc2  If the BOJ does change tactics, Sakakibara said, the impact would be mainly psychological, as it would be a move to a quantitative policy, a shift the BOJ Policy Board has long sternly rejected. \par \par \uc2  "The idea that the BOJ would shift to a quantitative easing policy by not sterilising would have a great psychological impact, rather than an actual impact on the economy," he said. \par \par \uc2  Monetary officials have denied such a shift is planned. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa last week denied a suggestion that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are ineffective because the BOJ subsequently sterilises them by draining funds from the money market. \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ is putting plenty of money into the market, so I don't agree with that reasoning," Miyazawa said. "This is a country with practically no interest rates," he said, meaning funds the BOJ leaves in the market are already sufficient to control interest rates. \par \par \uc2  BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami has also said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not thinking of using \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a tool of its monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  Despite such denials from officials, speculation persists in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market that the BOJ, if interest rates or the yen go higher, might move to unsterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Taking that policy is unrealistic now, when the markets are behaving calmly," said Noboru Yamaguchi, senior economist at Tokyo-Mitsubishi Securities. \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ has been clear about keeping a net surplus of about one trillion yen in the money market. In order to change that stance it would need a valid reason, such as steep rise in interest rates and the yen." \par \par \uc2  The BOJ acts as an agent of the Finance Ministry and conducts actual \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by having brokers and private banks buy or sell funds on its behalf. \par \par \uc2  Still, some analysts believe unsterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be risky and might also have little direct impact in boosting the economy or weakening the yen. \par \par \uc2  Targeting inflation or money supply increases, or buying government debt, would be either difficult to implement or risky, Kunio Okina, director of the BOJ's Institute for Monetary and Economic Policy Studies, said in a column in the Financial Times on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Okina said the BOJ would continue to consider further steps, however, including inflation targeting. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 5473-3748 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv8c028t2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
