\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar depressed despite gesture of U.S support. \b0\par\par\uc2 479 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 August 1999\par 03:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Aug 5 (Reuters) - The dollar recouped a fraction of its losses on Thursday after U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers affirmed the United States' belief in a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  In an interview with the BBC, he said a strong dollar was very much in the interest of the United States and he was always in contact with its G7 partners on markets. \par \par \uc2  However he also made a point of saying there was no point in trying to manage markets, which dealers took as sign the United States was not about to join \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the dollar. That left the dollar looking vulnerable, though they were still wary of unilateral action by the Bank of Japan. \par \par \uc2  By 0030 GMT the dollar had retreated to 114.10/15 yen from a 114.40 high immediately after Summer's remarks, leaving it well adrift of Tokyo's 115.09/12 close on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The euro was holding firm at $1.0775/80 after breaking above resistance at $1.0745 to a three-month high, but was having a tough time clearing the major $1.0800 level amid talk of euro selling by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Summers' comment about managing markets came only a day after Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa announced he had talked with Summers on the phone about \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  and they had agreed to keep in close contact. \par \par \uc2  The market had taken that as a hint the U.S. might join \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to restrain the yen, and a short-covering rally ensued which lifted the dollar as high as 115.80. \par \par \uc2  But on Thursday morning Miyazawa said the conversation with Summers had been general and did not deal with specifics, which left dealers thinking \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had not been discussed. \par \par \uc2  "This is getting to be a joke," said an exasperated dealer at a U.S. bank. "Who said what to whom, and what did that mean? The market's sick and tired of all this dancing around; they're going to take the dollar down and damn the MOF." \par \par \uc2  The next target for sellers was Monday's 113.95 trough, beyond which lay a big technical level at 113.55, a low from February 11. Only a rally above 114.80 resistance would negate the bearish pressure, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's woes were aggravated by further signs of an economic pick-up in Europe with French consumer confidence, service sector surveys in Germany and Italy and a British retail sales report all surprisingly strong. \par \par \uc2  This helped the euro clear resistance around $1.0745 to reach a three month high at $1.0796 and analysts were tipping a test of the May high around $1.0840 in the short term. ((Wayne Cole, Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 8584 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv8503a6k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
