\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE-Dollar bulls in retreat as euro beams. \b0\par\par\uc2 983 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 August 1999\par 17:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 5 (Reuters) - The dollar was bruised on Thursday amid renewed concern over fragile U.S. assets, while improved euro zone economic prospects boosted the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to new three-month peaks above $1.08. \par \par \uc2  Dollar bulls, who just a few weeks ago were anticipating a new era of dollar strength as the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was nearing decade's highs against European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , were hit by further economic evidence pointing to a recovery in Europe. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the yield premium of 10-year U.S. Treasury notes offer over German bonds was narrowing and said this was another positive for the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "We have seen a sharp turnaround in expectations for growth (in Europe) and feeding through from that there's been an equally sharp turnaround in interest rate expectations," said Keith Edmonds, chief analyst at IBJ international in London. \par \par \uc2  "Although there is still the possibility of rates increasing in the U.S., much brighter signs from European countries suggest their economies are starting to turn around. All this is hurting the dollar." \par \par \uc2  The euro rose as far as $1.0809, its highest since May 10, and was trading at $1.0802/08 at 1508 GMT. That compares with $1.0768/70 late in Europe on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Fears that an overheating of the U.S. economy could lead to capital flows out of U.S. assets had undermined the dollar, especially against the euro, said Neil Parker, treasury economist at the Royal Bank of Scotland. \par \par \uc2  The breach of the key $1.08 resistance level had shifted the market attention to the $1.10 target rather than parity with the dollar. Parker said more evidence was needed for the euro recovery to be sustained. \par \par \uc2  "I do not see parity being tested again," said Parker "But if we get two or three economic reports suggesting that the recovery (in Europe) is not going to be as fast as the market expects, you could see the euro back at $1.0550." \par \par \uc2  The euro was cheered earlier by a stronger-than-expected rise in June German industry orders. An encouraging report on business and consumer sentiment in the 11 nations of the euro zone added to optimism about the European economic outlook. \par \par \uc2  The weakness of the dollar was felt in various European quarters. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell below 1.48 Swiss francs for the first time in nearly four months and was last at 1.4805/15 against 1.4860/70 late on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell to its lowest in nearly six months against the yen as dealers were becoming less and less worried about possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action to curb yen strength. \par \par \uc2  Bank traders in London said expectation of an imminent joint U.S.-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had faded since U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers did not make any comment hinting at such a move. \par \par \uc2  Stop-loss orders placed below Monday's 113.80 trough accelerated the move down, but the dollar managed to climb back to 113.76/81 yen by late Europe as U.S. stocks reversed the initial losses, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was consolidating in subdued trading against both the dollar and the euro, failing to capitalise on the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness and showing little immediate reaction to news that the Bank of England's Monetary Policy Committee had left the key British repo rate unchanged at 5.0 percent. \par \par \uc2  The euro rose as far as $1.0809, its highest since May 10, and was trading at $1.0802/08 at 1508 GMT. That compares with $1.0768/70 late in Europe on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Fears that an overheating of the U.S. economy could lead to capital flows out of U.S. assets had undermined the dollar, especially against the euro, said Neil Parker, treasury economist at the Royal Bank of Scotland. \par \par \uc2  The breach of the key $1.08 resistance level had shifted the market attention to the $1.10 target rather than parity with the dollar. Parker said more evidence was needed for the euro recovery to be sustained. \par \par \uc2  "I do not see parity being tested again," said Parker "But if we get two or three economic reports suggesting that the recovery (in Europe) is not going to be as fast as the market expects, you could see the euro back at $1.0550." \par \par \uc2  The euro was cheered earlier by a stronger-than-expected rise in June German industry orders. An encouraging report on business and consumer sentiment in the 11 nations of the euro zone added to optimism about the European economic outlook. \par \par \uc2  The weakness of the dollar was felt in various European quarters. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell below 1.48 Swiss francs for the first time in nearly four months and was last at 1.4805/15 against 1.4860/70 late on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell to its lowest in nearly six months against the yen as dealers were becoming less and less worried about possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action to curb yen strength. \par \par \uc2  Bank traders in London said expectation of an imminent joint U.S.-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had faded since U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers did not make any comment hinting at such a move. \par \par \uc2  Stop-loss orders placed below Monday's 113.80 trough accelerated the move down, but the dollar managed to climb back to 113.76/81 yen by late Europe as U.S. stocks reversed the initial losses, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was consolidating in subdued trading against both the dollar and the euro, failing to capitalise on the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness and showing little immediate reaction to news that the Bank of England's Monetary Policy Committee had left the key British repo rate unchanged at 5.0 percent. \par \par \uc2  ((Lisa Jucca, London Capital Markets 0171 542 6745, elisabetta.jucca@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv85033hh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
