\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Euro/dollar hits new 3-month highs. \b0\par\par\uc2 547 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 1999\par 10:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 4 (Reuters) - The euro probed fresh three-month highs against the dollar in early European trading on Wednesday amid growing concerns over U.S. asset markets and optimism over the European economy. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was pressured by an underlying capital flow out of the United States, with fixed income markets in particular suffering from prospects of tighter Federal Reserve monetary policy and supply concerns. \par \par \uc2  "It (euro) doesn't look like it's going to rocket higher," said Jeff Woodruff, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BankBoston in London. "Any upward move around these levels is more on a flow basis than anything else." \par \par \uc2  At 0715 GMT, the euro stood at $1.0765/70 after hitting a peak of $1.0780 earlier, its highest level since May 11. It had been trading at $1.0695/97 late in Europe on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/Swiss fell to a trough of 1.4831 francs, its lowest since April 15 from 1.4945/50 late in Europe on Tuesday, also benefiting from the dollar's weakness. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was also approaching last Friday's $1.6250 2-1/2 month peak, last trading at $1.6225/30 against $1.6218/23 late on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Traders noted that positive economic data from Europe continued to fuel the euro's three-week-old recovery from its life lows near $1.01. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the day, the German Federal Statistics Office reported that German wholesale sales rose a real 1.2 percent in June from a year earlier while falling 0.7 percent year-on-year on a nominal basis. \par \par \uc2  French statistics office INSEE reported the nation's consumer confidence index rose to minus 8 in July from a June reading of minus 10. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the euro now faced technical resistance in the $1.08 area, the peaks in mid-May and mid-April. \par \par \uc2  The greenback's prevailing bearish sentiment kept dollar/yen near the 115-yen level, below which dealers became wary of pushing further down amid wariness of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar was last trading at 115.07/12 yen against 115.03/08 late on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the dollar was unable to sustain Tuesday's early rally to a peak of 115.66 yen in the absence of actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Tuesday's initial excitement was tempered by the U.S. Treasury's lukewarm response to Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa's comment about his telephone conversation with Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers, which the market had taken as a signal for possible joint U.S.-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, Miyazawa said the telephone call had been organised as the dollar appeared falling below 115 yen. \par \par \uc2  "The consensus in the market now is that 115 is the critical level for the Japanese authorities," a Japanese bank trader in London said, adding that the market could eventually push the dollar lower and test the authorities' resolve. \par \par \uc2  Summers was to speak at a news conference at 1300 GMT. Although the conference itself is for the Treasury's refunding, traders said they were waiting to see if he had anything to say on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well. ((Shin Kishima, London Capital Markets +44-171-542-6721, shinichi.kishima@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv84037ze\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
