\par\par \b \uc2  Nikkei extends loss, hit by Intenet stock declines. \b0\par\par\uc2 366 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 1999\par 06:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Aug 4 (Reuters) - The Tokyo stock market's benchmark Nikkei average extended its morning losses in early-afternoon trading on Wednesday, with market sentiment dampened by active selling of Internet-linked issues such as Softbank Corp . \par \par \uc2  Market dissappointment over inaction by Japan and U.S. authorities in defending the value of the dollar against the yen also spurred selling of blue-chip exporters, brokers said. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par As of 0348 GMT, the Nikkei average of 225 leading shares\tab    was down 296.24 points or 1.65 percent at 17,673.69, while September Nikkei futures\tab     were 260 points lower at 17,670.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Softbank and Fujitsu Ltd , both of which are viewed as core Internet stocks, declined sharply in reaction to the tumble overnight of U.S. Web-linked issues such as powerhouse Yahoo! Inc and American Online .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par As of 0359 GMT, Softbank was down nearly four percent, or 1,200 yen, at 29,600, breaking below the key 30,000 mark, while Fujitsu fell over three percent, or 110 yen, to 3,460 in active trading.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar rallied briefing on Tuesday after Japan's Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said Tokyo and Washington agreed that large \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fluctuations were unfavourable, stoking expectations of joint U.S.-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But the rally failed to be sustained amid a lack of any action between the two countries, despite a statement from Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that Miyazawa and U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers had spoken by telephone, which the U.S. Treasury Department later confirmed.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Summers himself remained mum about the possibility of coordinated action to dampen the yen's recent sharp rise.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar stood at just above 115.00 yen in Tokyo afternoon trade, down from 115.22/32 in late New York trading on Tuesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Active sales caught high-priced electronics manufacturers such as Sony Corp  and Matsushita Electric Industrial Co Ltd . As of 0411 GMT, Sony was off 1.87 percent to 14,170 yen, while Matsushita was down 3.61 percent at 2,670. ((Miki Shiogori, Tokyo Equities Desk +81-3 3432 9404 tokyo.equities.newsroom@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1999.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv84037ju\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
