\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Dollar rallies as Japan hints at U.S. help. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Wayne Cole \par \uc2 705 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 August 1999\par 09:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Aug 3 (Reuters) - The dollar enjoyed its first real bounce in three weeks of unrelenting pressure on Tuesday as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised the prospect of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the United States. \par \par \uc2  Right from the open \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials were telling anyone who would listen that the United States was also concerned by the rapid fall of the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The market was unimpressed at first but Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa took the unusual step of saying he had talked with U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers about \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday and they had agreed to keep in close touch. \par \par \uc2  "That was enough to convince us that perhaps it was time to book some profits," said a dealer at a U.S. bank, adding that the market was probably as short of the dollar as it had been since the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) began \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in June. \par \par \uc2  The dollar duly pushed through resistance at 115.00 and 115.30 yen, triggering stop-loss buying up to 115.53/58 yen by 0620 GMT, compared with 114.32/42 late in New York on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  That was a swift turnaround given the dollar's drop to a 5-month low around 113.90 yen on Monday, but dealers were unsure the rally would last unless the United States itself confirmed that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was an option. \par \par \uc2  "What we are waiting desperately for are any remarks from the U.S. side, rather than Japanese official comments," said Hideyuki Tsukamoto, manager at Fuji Bank's Investment Banking Products & Trading Group. \par \par \uc2  "All focus in the market now is on how the U.S. is seeing the recent fall in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , stocks and bonds," he said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. Treasury has been consistently critical of attempts by nations to manipulate \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so analysts reason it would have to have good reasons to change now. \par \par \uc2  One could be that a sustained slide in the dollar would threaten to spook offshore investors out of U.S. asset markets, although only last month Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan dismissed that scenario as fanciful. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers suspected the United States would want more effort from Japan to stimulate its domestic economy in return for any cooperation, even token, on dollar/yen rates. \par \par \uc2  This speculation permeated the Japanese government bond (JGB) market, where September JGB futures slumped 46 basis points to 129.74 and yields rose to 1.88 percent from 1.84 percent. \par \par \uc2  Whatever the truth, the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  scare gave the dollar a chance to improve its technical outlook, which was dire after its tumble from 122.80 yen in little more than a month. \par \par \uc2  Analysts expected 115.00 yen to provide a base now for a push to Fibonacci resistance at the 116.00 level. \par \par \uc2  Some saw scope for a steeper correction toward major resistance in the 117.30/60 yen area, given how short the market was, but even they doubted the longer-term downtrend was at an end and still expected to visit the next big bear targets at 113.50 yen and 111.30 yen. The short-covering fever also extended to euro/yen, which bounded as high as 123.45 yen from 122.16 in New York on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said, however, that Japanese institutions and exporters were quick to sell euros for hedging purposes and the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  faded to 122.88 late in the session, leaving them doubting it could clear resistance around 123.50. \par \par \uc2  The euro/dollar cross was sidelined by the antics in dollar/yen for most of the session and in late trade stood at $1.0650/57 against $1.0686/93 late in New York. Dealers here were surprised the euro had not made more capital out of a string of strong European economic data, the latest being an upbeat purchasing managers report for the whole euro area on Monday. Some said customers were wary of euro selling by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is on record as not wanting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rise too far and threaten export growth. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 8584 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv8303dk3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
