\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Dollar rally against yen stalls. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,090 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 August 1999\par 17:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 3 (Reuters) - The dollar rose sharply against the yen on Tuesday amid concern about possible joint U.S.-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but its rally stalled in Europe on scepticism about the likelihood and effectiveness of such action. \par \par \uc2  The dollar at one point had jumped more than 1.5 percent from Monday's five-month lows below 114 yen as comments by Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa raised the spectre of imminent coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action. \par \par \uc2  "Clearly the market is very much on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  alert, but the upside that we have seen in the aftermath of Mr. Miyazawa's comments has not been spectacular," said Neil MacKinnon, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Merrill Lynch in London. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was trading at 114.92/02 yen at 1457 GMT, up sharply from Monday's 113.80 five-month low but down from an intraday high of 115.66. \par \par \uc2  The early rally was fuelled by Miyazawa's statement that he and U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers had talked by telephone and agreed that rapid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements were undesirable and that they would keep in touch on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Treasury later confirmed the two leaders had talked, although it declined to comment on possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers said interest in selling dollars at higher levels, particularly among Japanese exporters and some investors, was overwhelming and the dollar would have a hard time extending gains in the absence of concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "The short term traders who bought dollars in anticipation of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  are not going to hold on to their positions for very long," said Akira Hoshino, chief trader at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi. \par \par \uc2  Even if the authorities heightened their rhetoric or actually stepped in to buy dollars for yen, many traders were sceptical about how long the market effect would last. \par \par \uc2  "They respect comments made by Mr. Miyazawa and they certainly would respect any comments made by Mr. Summers, but managing the dollar/yen exchange rate is proving to be a headache for the authorities," MacKinnon said. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the dollar's ability to hold the psychologically important 115-yen level would be the litmus test for the market's near-term directional bias. \par \par \uc2  The euro was mired in a narrow range in the mid-$1.06 area against the dollar, consolidating after its sharp run up to last week's 2-1/2 month highs. \par \par \uc2  The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was last trading at $1.0660/65 against $1.0668/70 late in Europe on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Hoshino at BTM said he expected the direction in which the euro next broke out of the $1.06-$1.07 consolidation band could determine if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was merely building a base for a further rally above $1.08 or whether its recent six percent rally from life lows near $1.01 had run its course. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the day, Germany's Ifo institute released growth forecasts for the euro zone and Germany, projecting a euro zone gross domestic product growth of 2.6 percent in 2000 after 2.0 percent in 1999, but the euro barely reacted to the Ifo numbers. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was at a standstill against most major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the market awaited the Bank of England's Monetary Policy Committee meeting scheduled on Wednesday and Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Sterling/dollar was last trading at $1.6160/63 against $1.6144/54 late in Europe on Monday, while euro/sterling was at 66.00/02 pence against 66.05/11 late on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/Swiss franc orbited the 1.50-franc area, last trading at 1.4986/96 against 1.4968/78 late on Monday. ((Shin Kishima, London Capital Markets +44-171-542-6721, shinichi.kishima@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  The early rally was fuelled by Miyazawa's statement that he and U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers had talked by telephone and agreed that rapid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements were undesirable and that they would keep in touch on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Treasury later confirmed the two leaders had talked, although it declined to comment on possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers said interest in selling dollars at higher levels, particularly among Japanese exporters and some investors, was overwhelming and the dollar would have a hard time extending gains in the absence of concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "The short term traders who bought dollars in anticipation of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  are not going to hold on to their positions for very long," said Akira Hoshino, chief trader at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi. \par \par \uc2  Even if the authorities heightened their rhetoric or actually stepped in to buy dollars for yen, many traders were sceptical about how long the market effect would last. \par \par \uc2  "They respect comments made by Mr. Miyazawa and they certainly would respect any comments made by Mr. Summers, but managing the dollar/yen exchange rate is proving to be a headache for the authorities," MacKinnon said. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the dollar's ability to hold the psychologically i